  {
    "text": "Page 68\nFIGURE 8.2  Thyrotoxicosis illustrating a typical thyroid stare\nTurner syndrome\nThe facial characteristics include ptosis—‘fish-like’ mouth, small chin (micrognathia), low-set\nears and deafness. Cardiac lesions include coarctation of the aorta and pulmonary stenosis.\nWebbing of the neck is the classic sign (CHAPTER 23\n).\nUraemic\nA sallow ‘muddy’ complexion with uraemic fetor—an ammoniacal halitosis.\nOther classic facies (similes)\nBulldog\nCongenital syphilis\nChipmunk\nThalassaemia major\nDeath mask\nPeritonitis; cholera\nElfin\nWilliam syndrome\nFish-like mouth\nTurner syndrome\nHatchet\nDystrophia myotonia\nLong face\nFragile X syndrome\nMask\nParkinson; Wilson disease\nMonkey\nHypopituitarism\nMoon\nCushing syndrome\nOld man in child\nMarasmus\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 198,
