      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": " Bacterial meningitis2\nBacterial meningitis is basically a childhood infection. Neonates and children aged 6–12 months\nare at greatest risk. Meningococcal disease can take the form of either meningitis or septicaemia\n(meningococcaemia) or both. Most cases begin as septicaemia, usually via the nasopharynx. The\nonset is usually sudden (see CHAPTER 89\n).\nClinical features (typical)\nInfancy\nFever, pallor, vomiting ± altered conscious and mental state\nLethargy\nIncreasing irritability with drowsiness\nRefusal to feed, indifference to mother\nNeck stiffness (not always present)\nCold extremities (a reliable sign)\nMay be bulging fontanelle\nKernig sign (see FIG. 20.1\n): unreliable\nBrudzinski sign (see FIG. 20.2\n): more reliable sign of meningeal irritation\nOpisthotonos (see FIG. 20.3\n): rare\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 494,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 791,
      "word_count": 114,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
      "author": "John",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "FIGURE 20.1  Kernig sign: pain in hamstrings with inability to straighten leg on\npassive knee extension with hip flexed at 90°\nFIGURE 20.2  Brudzinski sign: neck flexion causes involuntary flexion of hip\nand knee\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 495,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
