      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Page 267\nMain causes\nOutside obstruction (e.g. adhesions—commonest cause, previous laparotomy), strangulation in\nhernia or pockets of abdominal cavity (see FIG. 24.8\n)—this may lead to a ‘closed loop’\nobstruction.14\nLumen obstructions (e.g. foreign body, trichobezoar, gallstones, intussusception, malignancy).\nFIGURE 24.8  Operative findings (corrugated drainage material) in a 65-year-\nold man with subacute bowel obstruction after a 21-year history of nagging\nabdominal pain following a cholecystectomy\nClinical features\nSevere colicky epigastric and periumbilical (mainly) pain (see FIG. 24.9\n)\nSpasms every 3–10 minutes (according to level), lasting about 1 minute\nVomiting\nAbsolute constipation (nil after bowel emptied)\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 652,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 727,
      "word_count": 97,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
      "author": "John",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "No flatus\nAbdominal distension (esp. if lower SBO)\nFIGURE 24.9  Typical pain distribution of small bowel obstruction\nDxT colicky central pain + vomiting + distension → SBO\nSigns and tests\nPatient weak and sitting forward in distress\nVisible peristalsis, loud borborygmi\nAbdomen soft (except with strangulation)\nTender when distended\nIncreased sharp, tinkling bowel sounds\nDehydration rapidly follows, especially in children and elderly\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
