      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
      "author": "John",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Page 268\nDrip and suction\n Large bowel obstruction\nThe cause is commonly colorectal cancer (75% of cases), especially on the left side, but it can\noccur in diverticulitis or in volvulus of the sigmoid colon (10% of cases) and caecum.10 Sigmoid\nvolvulus is more common in older men and has a sudden and severe onset. The pain is less\nsevere than in SBO. Be wary of the non-surgical causes, simple constipation or acute pseudo-\nobstruction of the colon (Ogilvie syndrome). Consider ileus.\nClinical features\nSudden-onset colicky pain (even with cancer)\nEach spasm lasts less than 1 minute\nUsually hypogastric midline pain (see FIG. 24.10\n)\nVomiting may be absent (or late)\nAbsolute constipation (obstipation), no flatus\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 655,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 717,
      "word_count": 116,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
      "author": "John",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "FIGURE 24.10  Typical pain distribution of large bowel obstruction\nDxT colicky pain + distension ± vomiting → LBO\nSigns and tests\nIncreased bowel sounds, especially during pain\nDistension early and marked\nLocal tenderness and rigidity\nPR: empty rectum; may be rectosigmoid cancer or blood. Check for faecal impaction\nX-ray: distension of large bowel with separation of haustral markings, especially caecal\ndistension\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
