      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Page 280\nJoint swelling:\nacute (1–4 hours) with intense pain = blood infection or crystals (e.g. gout)\nsubacute (1–2 days) and soft = fluid (synovial effusion)\nchronic and bony = osteoarthritis\nchronic and soft/boggy = synovial proliferation\nA coarse crepitus suggests OA. Each joint should be examined specifically. Inspection should\nnote the presence of lumps or bumps such as Heberden nodes on the osteoarthritic DIP joints of\nthe hands, Bouchard nodes on the osteoarthritic PIP joints of the hands and rheumatoid nodules,\nwhich are the only pathognomonic finding of RA and gouty tophi. Signs that may be of\ndiagnostic help are presented in FIGURE 25.1\n.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 686,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 658,
      "word_count": 106,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
