    }
  },
  {
    "text": "FIGURE 26.4  Classification of haemorrhoids\nAnatomically there are three classical sites, namely 3, 7 and 11 o’clock (see FIG. 26.5\n).\nFIGURE 26.5  Three sites of primary haemorrhoids, looking into the anus from\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 742,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 212,
      "word_count": 33,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
      "author": "John",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Page 304\nbelow\nClinical stages and pathology2\nStage 1: First-degree internal haemorrhoids: three bulges form above the dentate line. Bright\nbleeding is common.\nStage 2: Second-degree internal haemorrhoids: the bulges increase in size and slide\ndownwards so that the person is aware of lumps when straining at stool, but they disappear\nupon relaxing. Bleeding is a feature.\nStage 3: Third-degree internal haemorrhoids: the pile continues to enlarge and slide\ndownwards, requiring manual replacement to alleviate discomfort. Bleeding is also a feature.\nStage 4: Fourth-degree internal haemorrhoids: prolapse has occurred and replacement of the\nprolapsed pile into the anal canal is impossible.\nSymptoms\nBleeding is the main and, in many people, the only symptom. The word ‘haemorrhoid’ means\nflow of blood. Other symptoms include prolapse, mucoid discharge, irritation/itching, tenesmus,\nincomplete bowel evacuation and pain (see FIG. 26.6\n).\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 743,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 941,
      "word_count": 137,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
      "author": "John",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "FIGURE 26.6  Severely prolapsed haemorrhoids requiring surgery\nTreatment\nInvasive treatment of haemorrhoids is based on three main procedures: rubber band ligation,\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 744,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 165,
      "word_count": 21,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
      "author": "John",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "cryotherapy and sphincterotomy. Injection is now not so favoured, while a meta-analysis\nconcluded that rubber band ligation was the most effective non-surgical therapy.9 Surgery is\ngenerally reserved for large strangulated piles. The best treatment, however, is prevention;\nsoftish bulky faeces that pass easily prevent haemorrhoids. People should be advised to have an\nadequate intake of non-caffeinated fluids and a diet with enough fibre by eating plenty of fresh\nfruit, vegetables, wholegrain cereals or bran. They should respond to the urge to defecate and\navoid straining at stool, complete their bowel action within a few minutes and avoid using\nlaxatives.\nAnal discharge\nAnal discharge refers to the involuntary escape of fluid from or near the anus. The causes may be\nconsidered as follows.5\n1. Continent\nAnal fistula\nPilonidal sinus\nSTIs: anal warts, gonococcal ulcers, genital herpes\nSolitary rectal ulcer syndrome\nCancer of anal margin\n2. Incontinent\nMinor incontinence—weakness of internal sphincter\nSevere incontinence—weakness of levator ani and puborectalis\n3. Partially continent\nFaecal impaction\nRectal prolapse\nAnal (faecal) incontinence\nAn Australian survey suggested 1 in 9 adults suffer some degree of faecal incontinence, which is\na common reason for institutionalisation of the elderly.10 Patients may be reluctant to seek\nmedical advice and doctors often do not ask specifically about the condition. The problem is as\ncommon in men as in women.\nApart from ageing, risk factors include perianal injury, such as childbirth injury, anal surgery,\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 745,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 1568,
      "word_count": 233,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
      "author": "John",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "irritable bowel syndrome and neurological disorders.\nIf there are symptoms of anal incontinence postnatally, early referral to a physiotherapist,\ncontinence nurse adviser or colorectal surgeon is advisable.11\nAmong the various treatments there are surgical possibilities, which vary from direct sphincter\nrepair, directed injections such as collagen and silicone into the anal sphincter, and an artificial\nanal sphincter (e.g. Acticon Neosphincter). A colostomy may be the last resort. It is worth\nkeeping in mind asking patients about the possibility of this problem and knowing ‘to whom to\nrefer’.\nRectal bleeding\nPatients present with any degree of bleeding from a smear on the toilet tissue to severe\nhaemorrhage. Various causes are presented in FIGURE 26.7\n. Common causes are polyps, colon\nand rectal cancer, ischaemic colitis, diverticular disease and haemorrhoids.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 746,
