      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Table 27.3\nkidney, adrenals and malignant melanoma.\nReticuloses such as Hodgkin lymphoma can involve the spine. Primary malignancies that\ndevelop in the vertebrae include multiple myeloma and sarcoma.\nBenign tumours to consider are often neurological in origin. The osteoid osteoma is aggravated\nby consuming alcohol and relieved by aspirin.\nThe tumours of the spine are summarised in TABLE 27.3\n.\nSignificant tumours affecting the thoracic and lumbar spine\nBenign\nMalignant\nOf bone\nOsteoid osteoma\nHaemangioma\nOsteoblastoma\nAneurysmal bone cyst\nEosinophilic granuloma\nPrimary:\nmultiple myeloma\nlymphomas (e.g. Hodgkin)\nsarcoma\nSpinal\nExtradural:\nlipoma\nneuroma\nfibroma\nIntradural:\nneuroma\nependymoma\nchordoma\nmeningioma\nSecondary:\nbreast\nlung\nprostate\nadrenals/kidney\nthyroid\nmelanoma\nDirect spread:\nstomach\nlarge bowel\npancreas\nuterus/cervix/ovary\nSource: Reproduced with permission from Kenna C, Murtagh J. Back Pain and Spinal Manipulation (2nd edn). Oxford: Butterworth-Heinemann,\n1997: 165–74.\nThe symptoms and signs that should alert the clinician to malignant disease are:\nback pain occurring in an older person\nunrelenting back pain, unrelieved by rest (this includes night pain)\nrapidly increasing back pain\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 760,
