    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Table 34.4\nPage 414\nCustard and cream (S.\naureus)\nNon-microbial food poisoning3\nFood (specific\ntypes)\nToxin\nOnset\nFeatures\n(symptoms)\nMushrooms\nMuscarine\nMinutes to hours\nN, V, D, P:\nCNS\nsymptoms\nToadstools\nAmatoxin\nHours\nN, V, D, P:\nhepatic\nfailure\nImmature or\nsprouting\npotatoes\nSolanine\nWithin hours\nN, V, D, P\nThroat\nconstriction\nFish\nIchthyosarcotoxin\nVarious (e.g.\nciguatera,\nscombrotoxin)\n10–60 minutes\n(occasionally\nlonger)\nN, V, D, P\nCircumoral\ntingling\nCNS\nsymptoms\nCollapse\nMussels\nMytilotoxism\n5–30 minutes\nN, V, P\nCNS:\nparalysis\nGrain, esp. rye\nErgot fungus\nAlphatoxin\nMinutes to 24\nhours\nN, V, P\nCirculatory\nand CNS\nFava beans\n(favism)\nEnzyme deficiency\nRapid\nV, D\nAcute\nhaemolysis\nN = nausea; V = vomiting; D = diarrhoea; P = abdominal pain\nA drug history is relevant, as is a family history of diarrhoea, which may be significant for\ncoeliac disease, Crohn disease and cystic fibrosis.\n",
