  {
    "text": "Table 34.6\nTable 34.7\nLiquid and uniform\nSmall bowel disorder (e.g. gastroenteritis)\nLoose with bits of faeces\nColonic disorder\nWatery, offensive, bubbly\nGiardia lamblia infection\nLiquid or semiformed, mucus ± blood\nEntamoeba histolytica\nBulky, pale, offensive\nMalabsorption\nPellets or ribbons\nIrritable bowel syndrome\nStool features as an aid to diagnosis\nStool appearance\nCause to consider\nChina clay\nObstructive jaundice\nBlack stool\nMelaena (blood) in faeces\nPea soup\nTyphoid fever\nRabbit pellets\nIrritable bowel syndrome\nRedcurrant jelly\nIntussusception\nRice water\nCholera\nSilver stool\nCarcinoma of ampulla of Vater\nToothpaste\nHirschsprung disease\nDistinction between small and large\nbowel diarrhoea\nSmall bowel\nLarge bowel\nVolume\nLarge\nSmall\nPain\nCentral\nLower/LIF\nBorborygmi\n+ +\n-\nUndigested food\n+\n-\nSteatorrhoea\n+/-\n-\nBlood\n-\n+\nMucus\n-\n+\nUrgency\n-\n+\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 1013,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 858,
      "word_count": 127,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
