      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Page 513\nQuotidian fever: The fever recurs daily. Morning spikes are characteristic of Pseudomonas\ninfection (e.g. pulmonary superinfection); afternoon spikes are indicative of cytomegalovirus\ninfection; and evening spikes suggest localised collection of pus (e.g. empyema of the gall\nbladder).\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 1240,
