      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "FIGURE 53.1  Important causes of arm pain (excluding trauma and arthritis)\nSoft tissue disorders of the elbow are extremely common, especially tennis elbow. Two types of\ntennis elbow are identifiable: ‘backhand’ tennis elbow, or lateral epicondylar tendinopathy, and\n‘forehand’ tennis elbow, or medial epicondylar tendinopathy, which is known also as golfer’s or\npitcher’s elbow.\nOther significant elbow disorders include inflammatory disorders of the elbow joint, such as\nrheumatoid arthritis, osteoarthritis and olecranon bursitis, which may follow recurrent trauma,\ngout, rheumatoid arthritis or infection.\nAnother important group of disorders are the various regional pain syndromes around the wrists,\nincluding the common de Quervain tenosynovitis (affecting the tendons of extensor pollicis\nbrevis and abductor pollicis longus) and to a lesser extent the extensor tendons to the fingers.\nPain from these overuse syndromes can be referred in a retrograde manner into the forearm.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 1504,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 985,
      "word_count": 140,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
