  {
    "text": "Table 56.1\nKnee pain can be referred from the lumbosacral spine. People with disc lesions may notice\nthat sitting, coughing or straining hurts the knee, whereas walking does not.\nFIGURE 56.1  Possible area of referred pain from disorders of the hip joint\nL3 nerve root pressure from an L2–3 disc prolapse (uncommon) and L4 nerve root pain will\ncause anteromedial knee pain; L5 reference from an L4–5 disc prolapse can cause anterolateral\nknee pain, while S1 reference from an L5–S1 prolapse can cause pain at the back of the knee (see\nFIG. 56.1\n).\nA diagnostic approach\nA summary of the diagnostic strategy model is presented in TABLE 56.1\n.\nThe painful knee: diagnostic strategy model\nProbability diagnosis\nLigament strains and sprains ± traumatic synovitis\nOsteoarthritis\n",
