    "text": " 1\n 2\n 3\n 4\n 5\n 6\n 7\n 8\n 9\nPSVT is rarely caused by organic heart disease in young patients.\nArrhythmia of sudden onset suggests PSVT, atrial flutter/fibrillation or ventricular\ntachycardia.\nA normal ECG in sinus rhythm does not exclude an accessory pathway.\nConsider conduction disorders such as the WPW syndrome in PSVT. Avoid\ndigoxin in WPW syndrome.\nCommon triggers of premature beats and PSVT are smoking, anxiety and caffeine\n(especially 8 or more cups a day).\nPatient education resource\nHand-out sheet from Murtagh’s Patient Education 8th edition:\nAtrial fibrillation\nReferences\nBoxall J. Annual update course for general practitioners. Course abstracts. Melbourne:\nMonash University, 2002: 16.\nLin SY et al. Association of arrhythmia in patients with cervical spondylosis: a nationwide\npopulation-based cohort study. J Clin Med, 2018 (23 Aug); 7(9): 236.\nDavis A, Bolin T, Ham J. Symptom Analysis and Physical Diagnosis (2nd edn). Sydney:\nPergamon, 1985.\nSandler G, Fry J. Early Clinical Diagnosis. Lancaster: MTP Press, 1986: 327–59.\nRobinson MJ, Roberton DM. Practical Paediatrics (5th edn). Melbourne: Churchill\nLivingstone, 2003: 501–2.\nMerriman A. Handbook of International Geriatric Medicine. Singapore: PG Publishing,\n1989: 99–100.\nAbumuaileq R. Atrial fibrillation in the old/very old: prevalence and burden, predisposing\nfactors and complications. E-Journal of Cardiology Practice, 13 Mar 2019; 17(1).\nBuckley N (Chair). Australian Medicines Handbook. Adelaide: Australian Medicines\nHandbook Pty Ltd, 2018: 286–9.\nO’Connor S, Baker T. Practical Cardiology. Sydney: MacLennan & Petty, 1999.\n",
