  {
    "text": "Page 776\nTable 64.3\nlevel of consciousness: degree of coma (see TABLE 64.1\n); the Glasgow coma scale (see\nTABLE 64.3\n) and AVPU scale (see TABLE 64.4\n) are frequently used as a guide to the\nconscious state\nskin features: look for evidence of injection sites (IV drug use, diabetes), snake bite\nmarks, colour (cyanosis, purpura, jaundice, rashes, hyperpigmentation) and texture\ncirculation\npulse oximetry\ntemperature: consider infection such as meningitis and hyperpyrexia if raised and hypothermia\n(e.g. hypothyroidism) if low\nhydration: dehydration may signify conditions such as a high fever with infections, uraemia,\nhyperglycaemic coma\nExamination\nAction\nIs the person breathing?\nIf not, clear airway and ventilate.\nNote chest wall movement.\nPerform cardiopulmonary\nresuscitation if necessary.\nCheck pulse and pupils.\nConsider naloxone.\nIs there evidence of trauma?\nConsider extradural haematoma.\nIs the person hypoglycaemic?\nObtain glucometer estimation of\nblood sugar.\nEvidence of diabetes\nAre vital functions present yet immediate\ncorrectable causes eliminated?\nPlace in coma position.\nGlasgow coma scale\nScore\nEye opening (E)\nSpontaneous opening\n4\nTo verbal command\n3\nTo pain\n2\nNo response\n1\nVerbal response (V)\nOrientated and converses\n5\n",
