    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Page 866\nTable 72.5\nOther treatments\nCorticosteroids (oral)\nThese can be very effective where other treatments or methods have failed. A 6–10-day\nshort course can be used. An example of a 6-day ‘rescue course’ is prednisolone 25, 25, 20, 15,\n10, 5 mg daily doses.\nIpratropium bromide (Atrovent)15\nThe nasal preparation of this topical anticholinergic is often very effective when rhinorrhoea is\nthe major problem.\nLeukotriene receptor antagonist\nRegarded as equivalent to oral antihistamines, they have a place in the management of children\nwith concurrent asthma and hay fever (e.g. montelukast).\nSurgery\nInferior turbinate reduction aims to reduce the size of turbinates and so reduce nasal obstruction\nwhen congested.\nGuidelines from elite task force16\n1. For initial treatment of seasonal allergic rhinitis in people aged 12 or older, routinely prescribe\nmonotherapy with an intranasal corticosteroid rather than an intranasal corticosteroid in\ncombination with an oral antihistamine.\n2. For initial treatment of seasonal allergic rhinitis in those aged 15 or more, recommend an\nintranasal corticosteroid over a leukotriene receptor antagonist.\n3. For treatment of moderate to severe seasonal allergic rhinitis in people 12 or older, you may\nrecommend the combination of an intranasal corticosteroid and an intranasal antihistamine\ninitially.\nTABLE 72.5\n summarises recommended steps in management.\nSummary of recommended treatment steps for allergic\nrhinitis10\nPatient education\nAllergen avoidance (if possible)\nMild cases:\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 2042,
