      "word_count": 345,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
      "author": "John",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "of the ACEIs and ARBs on the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone pathway system is illustrated in\nFIGURE 77.7\n. They have a similar adverse kidney and other effects to the ACE inhibitors but\ncough does not appear to be a significant adverse effect. This group is used for mild-to-moderate\nhypertension alone or with other antihypertensive agents. They are useful alternatives for patients\nwho discontinue an ACE inhibitor because of cough. They may be used in combination with\nthiazide diuretics.\nFIGURE 77.7  Drugs targeting the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 2147,
