    "text": "35–40\nClass II: moderately\nobese\nConsider medical therapy if >35\n≥40\nClass III: severely\nobese\nCombined program plus medical\ntherapy\nConsider gastric surgery\nFIGURE 80.1  Mortality vs body mass index (BMI) reference scale. Note the\n‘J’-shaped curve.\nOther anthropomorphic measurements\nWaist–hip circumference ratio (W/H ratio): a slightly better predictor of cardiovascular risk\nthan BMI. Obesity with a high waist–hip ratio (>1.0 in men and >0.9 in women) confers a\nsignificantly greater risk of diabetes, stroke, coronary artery disease and early death than a\nsimilarly obese BMI with a lower waist–hip ratio. Thus, abdominal fat (‘apple’ body shape,\n",
