    "text": "Table 83.2\nFIGURE 83.2  Normally a child will sit without support at 7–8 months\nSource: PeopleImages/iStock/Getty Images\nBeyond 12 months, children will expand this neurological connection into increasingly complex\nactivities (see TABLE 83.2\n).\nGross motor skills we want to see emerge (12 months to 6 years)\n2 years—walking up stairs holding onto an adult hand, and possibly attempting\nalternating feet with each stair\n2½ years—observing a child being able to jump\n3 years—starting to try and to use a tricycle\n5–6 years—weaning the trainer wheels off the bike\n6 years—skipping\nNote: Any loss of gross motor (or any other developmental) skill is a red flag.\nFine motor development\n",
