  {
    "text": "Table 86.4\nClinical features\nThe clinical features of rubella are presented in TABLE 86.4\n and FIGURE 86.5\n and the\ncomplications in TABLE 86.5\n.\nClinical features of rubella\nThere is no prodrome.\nA generalised, maculopapular rash, sometimes pruritic, may be the only evidence of\ninfection.\nOther symptoms are usually mild and short-lived.\nThere is often a reddened pharynx but sore throats are unusual. An exudate may be\nseen as well as palatal exanthem.\nFever is usually absent or low-grade.\nOther features: headache, myalgia, conjunctivitis and polyarthritis (small joints).\nLymphadenopathy may be noted; usually postauricular, suboccipital and\npostcervical.\nThe patient is infectious for a week before and at least 4 days after the onset of the\nrash.\nThe rash\nA discrete pale pink maculopapular rash (not confluent as in measles).\nStarts on the face and neck—spreads to the trunk and extremities.\nVariable severity: may be absent in subclinical infection.\nExaggerated on skin exposed to sun.\nBrief duration—usually fades on the third day.\nNo staining or desquamation.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 2327,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 1072,
      "word_count": 165,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
      "author": "John",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "postauricular\nlymphadenopathy\n\nrash first appea\non neck and fac\n\nscattered rash\non body\n\nFIGURE 86.5 Rubella: typical symptoms\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 2328,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 127,
