      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "mastitis carcinomatosa\nPitfalls (often missed)\nPregnancy\nChest wall pain (e.g. costochondritis)\nPectoralis muscle spasm\nReferred pain, esp. thoracic spine\nBornholm disease (epidemic pleurodynia)\nHerpes zoster\nMechanical:\nbra problems\npendulous breasts stretching Cooper ligaments\nweight change\ntrauma\nRarities:\nhyperprolactinaemia\nnerve entrapment\nmammary duct ectasia\nsclerosing adenosis\nankylosing spondylitis\nSeven masquerades checklist\nDepression\nDrugs (e.g. OCP, HRT, marijuana)\nSpinal dysfunction\nIs the patient trying to tell me something?\nYes. Fear of malignancy. Consider psychogenic causes.\nProbability diagnosis\nIn the non-pregnant patient, generalised pain, which may be cyclical or non-cyclical, is\ncommonest. Typical patterns are illustrated in FIGURE 93.1\n.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 2494,
