      "char_count": 1648,
      "word_count": 253,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
      "author": "John",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "uterine artery embolisation\nendometrial ablation (provided no contraindications)\nhysterectomy (appropriate for women with increased endometrial cancer risk, e.g. endometrial\nhyperplasia)\nPractice tip\nAcute severe uterine bleeding:10\ntranexamic acid 1–1.5 g (o) 6–8 hourly until bleeding stops\nor\nnorethisterone 5–10 mg (o) 4 hourly until bleeding stops\nor\nmedroxyprogesterone 10 mg (o) 4 to 8 hourly until bleeding stops\nor\nethinyloestradiol 30–35 mcg combined oral contraceptive pill, 6 hourly until\nbleeding stops, re-evalute after 48 hours\n Uterine fibroids (leiomyoma)\nFibroids are benign tumours of smooth muscle of the myometrium. They are usually classified\naccording to their location: submucosal, intramural, subserosal and cervical. They are oestrogen-\ndependent and shrink with the onset of menopause.\nClinical features\nAffects 40–80% of women by age 50 years\nOnly 1 in 800 develop malignancy\nUsually asymptomatic\nSymptoms\nOften asymptomatic if small\nMenorrhagia\n",
