    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Table 105.1\nadolescent males who should be reassured that they are normal variants. They are not\npremalignant and require no treatment.14\nFordyce spots of the penis: refer to CHAPTER 104\n.\nFIGURE 105.7  Pearly penile papules\n Penile ulcers\nA common cause of penile ulcers is trauma, related to sexual activity, to the frenulum if it is\ncongenitally tight. Such traumatic ulcers may be slow to heal and the frenulum may need\nsurgical division. The ulcers may resemble a venereal ulcer (e.g. syphilitic chancre or herpes\nsimplex). Another important (although rare) cause is cancer of the penis. Various causes are\nlisted in TABLE 105.1\n.\nCauses of penile lesions\n",
