    "text": "Page 1179\nConsider bladder cancer if persistent dysuria.\n Lower urinary tract symptoms (LUTS)\nThese symptoms can be grouped as voiding symptoms (obstructive) or storage symptoms\n(irritative).7 Irritative symptoms may be caused by a bladder problem only. Obstructive\nsymptoms are usually caused by the prostate (which can also cause irritative symptoms). The old\nterm ‘prostatism’ is ill defined and best dropped.\nVoiding (obstructive) symptoms\nHesitancy\nWeak stream\nPostmicturition dribble or irregular stream\nIncomplete emptying/urinary retention\nStraining\nStorage (irritative) symptoms\nUrgency\nUrge incontinence\nFrequency\nNocturia\nSuprapubic pain\n Bladder outlet obstruction (BOO)\nSymptoms of BOO are present in most men after the age of 60 years. The commonest cause is\nbenign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH). BPH is a histological diagnosis that, strictly, should not be\nused for symptoms. Only a minority of patients require surgery for relief of obstructive\nsymptoms. Bladder outlet obstruction can also be caused by bladder neck obstruction and\nurethral sphincter spasm (see FIG. 106.1\n).\n",
