    "text": " 1\n 2\n 3\n 4\n 5\n 6\n 7\n 8\nantibiotics (especially penicillins), thiazides, anti-epileptics, allopurinol and\nNSAIDs.\nPatient education resources\nHand-out sheets from Murtagh’s Patient Education 8th edition:\nHerpes simplex (cold sores)\nHerpes zoster (shingles)\nKawasaki syndrome\nMeasles\nPityriasis rosea\nRubella\nVaricella (chicken pox)\nReferences\nHunter JAA, Savin JA, Dahl MV. Clinical Dermatology (3rd edn). Oxford: Blackwell\nScientific Publications, 2002: 64.\nMurtagh J. Patient Education (7th edn). Sydney: McGraw-Hill, 2016.\nThomas RM. Drug eruptions. Med Int, 1988; 49: 2038–42.\nCutaneous drug reactions [published 2015]. In Therapeutic Guidelines [digital].\nMelbourne: Therapeutic Guidelines Limited; 2015. www.tg.org.au, accessed April 2021.\nShingles [published 2019]. In: Therapeutic Guidelines [digital]. Melbourne: Therapeutic\nGuidelines Limited; 2019. www.tg.org.au, accessed April 2021.\nPain associated with shingles (herpes zoster) [published 2020]. In: Therapeutic Guidelines\n[digital]. Melbourne: Therapeutic Guidelines Limited; 2020. www.tg.org.au, accessed\nApril 2021.\nDwyer DE, Cunningham AL. Herpes simplex and varicella zoster virus infection. Med J\nAust, 2002; 177: 267–72.\nInfected skin [published 2015]. In: Therapeutic Guidelines [digital]. Melbourne:\nTherapeutic Guidelines Limited; 2015. www.tg.org.au, accessed April 2021.\n",
