    "text": "FIGURE 115.2  (a) Parts of an ulcer, (b) types of ulcer\nSource: Davis et al. Symptom Analysis and Physical Diagnosis (2nd edn), page 309. Reproduced with permission from Pergamon\nPress.\nFloor of the ulcer\nThe floor or base of the ulcer provides useful clinical information. A dry or extended base or\nnecrotic eschar in the floor implies ischaemia. Venous ulcers, on the other hand, are often\nsuperficial and tend to have fibrinous exudate and ooze, sometimes purulent fluid.\nColour guide:\n",
