      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Page 1305\nFIGURE 116.26  Application of liquid nitrogen with cotton wool applicator: (a)\napplicator too large, (b) correct size and approach of applicator, (c) correct size\nbut wrong position of applicator\nMethod B: cryotherapy spray method\nSpraying liquid nitrogen under high pressure (the timed spot freeze open-spray method) is the\nmost effective method of cryotherapy. It produces sufficient intense cold to treat deeper lesions.\nExplain likely reaction to patient, such as the appearance of blisters (possibly blood blisters). The\noptimal time for retreatment of warts is in 2–3 weeks (not longer than 3 weeks).\nBiopsies\nThere are various methods for taking biopsies from skin lesions. These include scraping, shaving\nand punch biopsies, all of which are useful but not as effective or safe as excisional biopsies.\nShave biopsies\nThis simple technique is generally used for the tissue diagnosis of premalignant lesions and some\nmalignant tumours, but not melanoma.\nMethod\n1. Infiltrate with LA.\n2. Holding a number 10 or 15 scalpel blade horizontally, shave off the tumour just into the upper\ndermis (see FIG. 116.27\n).\n3. Diathermy may be required for haemostasis.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 3068,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 1171,
      "word_count": 183,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
      "author": "John",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "FIGURE 116.27  Shave biopsy\nThe biopsy site usually heals with minimal scarring.\nPunch biopsy\nThis biopsy (see FIG. 116.28\n) has considerable use in general practice where full-thickness skin\nspecimens are required for histological diagnosis. (Good quality disposable biopsy punches are\navailable from Dermatech Laboratories.)\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 3069,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 327,
      "word_count": 45,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
      "author": "John",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Page 1306\nFIGURE 116.28  Punch biopsy\nSteroid injections into skin lesions\nSuitable lesions for steroid injections are:\nplaque psoriasis\ngranuloma annulare\nhypertrophic scars\nkeloid scars (early development)\nalopecia areata\nchondrodermatitis nodularis helicis\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 3070,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 260,
      "word_count": 32,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
      "author": "John",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "lichen simplex chronicus\nnecrobiosis lipoidica\nhypertrophic lichen planus\norf and milker’s nodules\nTriamcinolone is the appropriate long-acting corticosteroid (10 mg/mL). It may be diluted in\nequal quantities with saline.\nMethod\n1. The steroid should be injected into the lesion (not below it).\n2. Insert a 25 or (preferably) 27 gauge needle, firmly locked to a small insulin-type 1 mL syringe,\ninto the lesion at the level of the middle of the dermis (see FIG. 116.29\n).\n3. High pressure is required with some lesions (e.g. keloid).\n4. Inject sufficient steroid to make the lesion blanch.\n5. Several sites will be needed for larger lesions, so preceding LA may be required in some\ninstances. Avoid infiltration of steroid in larger lesions: use multiple injections.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 3071,
      "type": "drug_info",
      "char_count": 767,
      "word_count": 123,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
      "author": "John",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "FIGURE 116.29  Injection of corticosteroid into mid-dermis\nElliptical excisions\nSmall lesions are best excised as an ellipse. Generally, the long axis of the ellipse should be\nalong the skin tension lines identified by natural wrinkles.\nThe intended ellipse should be drawn on the skin (see FIG. 116.30\n). The placement will depend\non such factors as the size and shape of the lesion, the margin required (usually 3 mm) and the\nskin tension lines.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 3072,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 448,
      "word_count": 74,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
      "author": "John",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "FIGURE 116.30  Ellipse excision\nGeneral points13\nThe length of the ellipse should be three times the width.\nThis length should be increased (say, to four times) in areas with little subcutaneous tissue\n(dorsum of hand) and high skin tension (upper back).\nA good rule is to obtain an angle at each end of the excision of 30° or less.\nThese rules should achieve closure without ‘dog ears’.\nExcisions on the face\nIt is important to select optimal sites for elliptical excisions of tumours of the face. As a rule it is\nbest for incisions to follow wrinkle lines and the direction of hair follicles in the beard area.\nTherefore, follow the natural wrinkles in the glabella area, the ‘crow’s feet’ around the eye and\nthe nasolabial folds (see FIG. 116.31\n). To determine non-obvious wrinkles, gently compress the\nrelaxed skin in different directions to demonstrate the lines.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 3073,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 870,
      "word_count": 147,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
      "author": "John",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "FIGURE 116.31  Recommended lines for excisions on face\nSource: Adapted from JS Brown, Minor Surgery: A Text and Atlas. London: Chapman & Hall, 1986\nFor tumours under 1 cm on the forehead, make horizontal incisions. Vertical incisions may be\nused for large tumours of the forehead. Ensure that you keep your incisions in the temporal area\nquite superficial, as the frontal branch of the facial nerve is easily cut.\nWhen to refer\n13\nReferral should be considered for:\nuncertainty of diagnosis\nsuspicion of melanoma\ntumours larger than 1 cm\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 3074,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 538,
      "word_count": 89,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
      "author": "John",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Page 1307\nrecurrent tumours, despite treatment\nincompletely excised tumours, especially with poor healing\ndoubts about appropriate treatment\nrecommended treatment beyond skills of practitioner\nfrequent multiple tumours (e.g. organ transplant patients)\nSCC on the lip or ear\ninfiltrating or scar-like morphoeic BCC, particularly those on the nose or around the nasal\nlabial fold\ncosmetic concerns, such as lesions in the upper chest and upper arms where keloid scarring is\na potential problem\nKey points\nUncomplicated small tumours are best removed by an elliptical excision with a\n3 mm margin for BCC and a 4 mm margin for SCC.\nCaution should be used in the management of tumours on the face, including the\nears and lips.\nPatient education resources\nHand-out sheets from Murtagh’s Patient Education 8th edition:\nFatty tumour (lipoma)\nMolluscum contagiosum\nSeborrhoeic keratoses\nSkin cancer\nWarts\nResource\nHayes M. Skin Cancer, Melanoma and Mimics. Brisbane: Skin Cancer Books, 2013.\n",
