  {
    "text": "Back Cover\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 3784,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 11,
      "word_count": 2,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
      "author": "John",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "~ MURTAGH’S\n\nSold worldwide and translated into 13 languages, John Murtagh’s General Practice is widely\nrecognised as the gold standard reference for general practice and primary health care. Its emphasis\non the importance of clinical reasoning, early diagnosis and treatment makes this the essential\nreference for medical students, trainees and general practitioners\n\nWritten by renowned general practitioners and educators, and with all content reviewed for currency\n\nby leading experts, the eighth edition provides fundamental knowledge and skills required for the\nchallenging field of general practice\n\nKey features:\nDiagnostic strategy models for common presenting problems, including diagnostic triads\nClinical frameworks, including management and treatment\nEvidence-based research, with all content reviewed for currency by leading experts\nExtensive coverage of mental health, health promotion, women’s health, and paediatric\nand geriatric care\nNew in this edition:\nNew chapters on obesity, mood disorders, breast disorders, and traveller's health and tropical\nmedicine\n\nUpdated and new information on genetic disorders, chronic pain and infectious diseases,\nincluding acute respiratory distress syndrome with reference to coronaviruses and COVID-19\n\nRestructure of table of contents to allow for easier navigation\n\nis Emeritus Professor in General Practice at Monash Unive: Professional Fellow of General\nPractice at the University of Melbourne; and Adjunct Clinical Professor in the Graduate School of Medicine,\nUniversity of Notre Dame\n\nis a general practitioner and was Adjunct Senior Lecturer in the Schoo! of Primary Health Care at\nMonash University. She received a Distinguished Service Award of the RACGP in 2014. Jill has a wealth of rural\nand urban medical experience\n\nis a general practitioner in the Tiwi islands and was Senior Lecturer at Flinders University. He's\n\non the Editorial Advisory Board of Diabetes Management Journal, a member of Choosing Wisely Australia (NPS)\nand a prolific writer for a number of publications.\n\nis a general practitioner in Sydney. She holds postgraduate qualifications in Dermatology\nMedica! Education, and Sexual and Reproductive Health\n\nISBN 9781743768235\n\n9 I\n\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "John Murtagh General Practice, 8th Edition.pdf",
      "page": 3785,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 2217,
      "word_count": 316,
      "title": "John Murtagh General Practice",
      "author": "John",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": "8th"
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Color Atlas of Skin Diseases \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 1,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 30,
      "word_count": 5,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Table of Contents \n1. \nAcne \nRosacea \n2. \nBacterial Infections \nFolliculitis \nImpetigo \n3. \nBenign Neoplasms \nSeborrheic Keratoses \nGranuloma Pyogenicum \nLentigo Simplex \n4. \nChildhood \nInfectious \nDisea­\nses/skin Lesions \nVaricella (Chicken Pox) \nHand, Foot and Mouth Disease \nVerruca Plana \n5. \nEczematous Dermatitis \nPityriasis Rosea \nVesicular Hand Dermatitis \nSeborrheic Dermatitis \nNummular Dermatitis \n6. \nFungal Infections \nTinea Capitis \nTinea Versicolor \nCandidiasis \n7. \nGyrate Erythema \nErythema Chronicum Migrans (Lyme \nDisease) \n8. Pre-malignant and Malignant Les­\nions \nActinic Keratoses \nBasal Cell Carcinoma \nSquamous Cell Carcinoma \nMalignant Melanoma \nAtypical Mole (Dysplastic) \nAtypical Mole \nAtypical Mole \nAtypical Mole \n9. \nPsoriasis \nPsoriasis of the Nails \nIntertriginous Psoriasis \nPsoriasis of the Scalp \nPustular Psoriasis \nGuttate Psoriasis \n10. Sexually Transmitted Diseases \nHerpes Simplex, Penis \nHerpes Simplex, Vulva \nHerpes Simplex, Perineum \nHerpes Simplex in AIDS \nCondyloma Acuminatum (Genital \nWarts) \nSecondary Syphilis \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 2,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 1062,
      "word_count": 128,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "11. Stings and Insect Bites \nScabies \nPediculosis (Lice) \n12. Urticaria \nPapular Urticaria \nUrticaria \n13. Viral Infections \nMolluscum Contagiosum \nHerpes Simplex \nHerpes Zoster \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 3,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 179,
      "word_count": 22,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rosacea \nRosacea is a congestive blushing and \nflushing reaction of the central areas of \nthe face. It is usually associated with an \nacneiform \ncomponent \n(papules, \npustules, and oily skin). It usually \noccurs in middle-aged and older people. \nThe cheeks, nose, and chin, on the \nentire face, may have a rosy hue. \nBurning or stinging often accompanies \nepisodes of flushing. It is much more \ncommon than lupus erythematosus, with which it is often confused. Rosacea \nis distinguished from acne by age, the presence of the vascular component, and \nthe absence of comedones. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 4,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 577,
      "word_count": 91,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Folliculitis \nFolliculitis is characterized by red-ringed \npapules and pustules at hair follicles. Gram­\nnegative folliculitis may be spread by \ncontaminated hot tubs. Gram stain and culture \nwill help to differentiate bacterial from non­\nbacterial folliculitis. History is important for \npinpointing the cause of non-bacterial \nfolliculitis. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 5,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 344,
      "word_count": 45,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Impetigo \nSuperficial honey-colored serous crusts are \ncharacteristic of this disorder. It is usually \ncaused by a staphylococcus infection. Culture is \nrarely reliable. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 6,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 171,
      "word_count": 22,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Seborrheic Keratoses \nThese lesions are benign overgrowths of \nepithelium, largely appearing on the torso, \nface, and neck. They are seen on almost every­\none over the age of 50. The borders are \ntypically irregular, and they range in color \nfrom beige or gray-white to very dark brown. \nThese \"barnacles\" of older skin can number \nonly a few to as many as hundreds. Although \noften raised and dry, they can be flatter and \ngreasier (seborrheic) in texture. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 7,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 459,
      "word_count": 76,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Granuloma Pyogenicum \nThis is a vascular reactive nodule that develops as \na response to a minor injury. The overgrowth of \ncapillaries leads to a raised red lump which bleeds \nprofusely when torn. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 8,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 199,
      "word_count": 32,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Lentigo Simplex \nThese lesions occur on sun-exposed skin, especially \nface, arms, and hands. Lesions are flat, and \npigmented in shades of brown, with characteristically \nsharp borders. They tend to fade with sun avoidance. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 9,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 225,
      "word_count": 33,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Varicella \nChicken Pox  \nThe rash is pruritic and most prominent on the face, \nscalp and trunk. It appears as multitudes of red­ \nringed papules and vesicles in varying stages of \ndevelopment. Crusts eventually form and slough off \nin 7 to 14 days. Nondermatomal distribution and \nlesions of varying stages distinguish primary \nvaricella from herpes zoster. Fever and malaise may be mild in children and \nmuch more severe in adults. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 10,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 434,
      "word_count": 69,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Hand, Foot, and Mouth Disease \nThe disorder is characterized by stomatitis and \nvesicular rash on palms of hands and soles of feet. It \nis caused by Coxsackieviruses A5, 10, 16. The \ndevelopment of mouth sores is most troublesome to \nadults. The skin lesions are vesicopustules, 0.5 to 5 \nmm, red-ringed, more oval than round, on palms, \nsides of fingers and soles. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 11,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 367,
      "word_count": 61,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Verruca Plana \nThe numerous discrete lesions, closely set, usually \noccur on face, dorsa of hands and shins. Lesions are \nflat-topped, slightly elevated, well demarcated, \ngenerally flesh-colored, with a matte-smooth surface. \nLesions tend to spontaneously disappear. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 12,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 269,
      "word_count": 35,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Pityriasis Rosea \nThis disorder is a common, but unexplainable, \nreaction. The initial lesion, \"herald patch\", is red and \nscaly, followed in 1 to 2 weeks by widespread, oval, \nscaling, fawn-colored macules 4 to 5 mm in \ndiameter over the trunk and proximal extremities. \nPityriasis rosea is usually an acute self-limiting \nillness that lasts 4 to 8 weeks. It is not highly infectious. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 13,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 387,
      "word_count": 62,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Vesicular Hand Dermatitis \nThis disorder is a severely pruritic reaction in \nindividuals with a personal or family history of \nallergic manifestations. It is characterized by flares \nof congestion resulting in deep and superficial \nblisters, followed by peeling, scaling, and a dry, \nreddened surface. Flares generally result from \ncontact with irritants, but stress is also a significant \nfactor. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 14,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 399,
      "word_count": 57,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Seborrheic Dermatitis \nSeborrheic dermatitis is generally limited to the scalp; \nhowever, dry scales and underlying erythema can \noccur on the face, ears, chest, back, and body folds. \nSkin may be dry or oily. In infants, a widespread \nreaction is associated with minimal discomfort. The \nyeast organism, Pityrosporum, may be a factor. Mild \nscaling without any erythema is often termed simple \ndandruff. Tinea capitis may simulate dandruff or seborrheic dermatitis, and \nscrapings should be taken for KOH examination and fungal culture, especially \nin children, if hair loss is present. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 15,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 589,
      "word_count": 88,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Nummular Dermatitis \nA pruritic dermatosis, characterized by round to oval \n(coin-shaped) areas of vesiculation, superficial \ncrusting, and redness. Number of lesions varies from \nfew to many. More often this is a symmetrical \npattern in young adults. Not related to atopic \ndermatitis. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 16,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 288,
      "word_count": 41,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Tinea Capitis \nAlong with hair loss, the scalp surface shows \nseborrheic dermatitis-like scaling, impetigo-like \ncrusting, pustules, inflammatory nodules or kerion. \nIdentify tinea with KOH culture onto a fungal media. \nNo longer a disease confined to children. If infection \nsuspected, all family members should be examined. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 17,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 327,
      "word_count": 45,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Tinea Versicolor \nAsymptomatic to mildly itchy macules that scale \nreadily on scraping. Lesions, usually occur on the \ntrunk, but may appear on upper arms, neck, face, \nand groin. Caused by a yeast organism, \nPityrosporum orbiculare. Altered pigmentation can \nbe very subtle to obvious, both hypo and \nhyperpigmented. KOH shows characteristic spores and hyphae. Fungal culture \nis not useful. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 18,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 394,
      "word_count": 58,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Candidiasis \nCommon normal flora, but it may become an \nopportunistic pathogen widespread in patients with \nAIDS and other immunosuppressed patients. \nMucocutaneous candidiasis occurs on the vulva, anus, \nbreast or groin folds. Superficial denuded beefy red \nareas \nwith \nor \nwithout \nscattered \nsatellite \nvesicopustules with marginal scaling. Microscopic examination with 10% \nKOH reveals budding spores and short hyphae. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 19,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 425,
      "word_count": 56,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Erythema Chronicum Migrans \nLyme Disease  \nCaused by the spirochete Borrelia burgdorferi, which \nis transmitted to humans by a deer tick bite, infection, \nis characterized by erythema migrans. A flat or \nslightly raised red lesion appears at the site. The \nreaction can become quite large, is generally circular \nin shape, and can show several concentric rings \n(target pattern). Erythema migrans is often accompanied by flu-like illness \nwith fever, chills, and myalgias. At this stage, laboratory tests are not reliable. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 20,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 524,
      "word_count": 78,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Actinic Keratoses \nActinic keratoses are single or multiple, flesh­\ncolored or slightly hyperpigmented, dry, rough, \nscaly lesions which occur on skin exposed to the \nsun. Cells are atypical, and they are considered to \nbe pre-malignant because some may eventually \nbecome squamous cell cancers. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 21,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 297,
      "word_count": 43,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Basal Cell Carcinoma \nThis lesion represents 90% of skin cancers. Basal \ncell carcinoma is the most common cancer. On the \nface, it usually starts as a reddened papule or nodule \nwith a smooth surface and a translucent, pearly \nquality. Because of a poorly formed stroma, it is \nfragile and often bleeds. On the torso, the lesion has \nan irregular surface, bright red color, sometimes scaly, with a distinct edge. \nHistologic examination is required. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 22,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 452,
      "word_count": 73,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Squamous Cell Carcinoma \nThis lesion usually appears on skin that shows other \nsignificant changes of chronic sun exposure. \nEspecially prevalent in fair-skinned people who \nsunburn easily and tan poorly. It may arise out of \nactinic keratoses. Characteristically, the lesion \nappears fairly rapidly as a small red, conical, hard \nnodule. Should it appear on the mucus membrane or lip area, it behaves much \nmore aggressively and can be fatal. Histologic examination is required. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 23,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 481,
      "word_count": 72,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Malignant Melanoma \nRecognized through the mnemonic, \"A-B-C-D:\" \nAsymmetry of contour, irregularity of Border and \nColor, and Diameter larger than 6 mm. Melanomas \nvary from macules to nodules. Color ranges from \nflesh tints to pitch black and mixtures of white, blue, \npurple, and red. Any pigmented skin lesion with \nrecent change in appearance should be suspected. \nMalignant melanoma can exist in a superficial spreading mode for years and \nstill be curable by excision with 1 to 2 cm margins. Once a vertical growth \nphase develops, rapid spread through blood and lymph vessels occurs. \nHistologic examination is required. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 24,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 629,
      "word_count": 96,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Atypical Moles \nDysplastic \nchange \nimplies \nabnormal \ncell \ndevelopment, which does not necessarily imply \nprecancerous change. These atypical moles, show \nirregular outlines, and different shades and patterns \nof brown color. If they appear in a person with a \nfamily history of melanoma and are multiple in \nnumber, the incidence of cancer developing reaches \n100%. If they are sporadic in pattern and number, \nthey should be photographed and reexamined \nregularly. Histopathologic examination is required. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 25,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 511,
      "word_count": 72,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Psoriasis of the Nails \nPitting of nail surface with spots of white to yellow­\nbrown (oil droplets) reflects psoriatic changes in the \nnail matrix and nail bed respectively. Distally, there \nare irregular onycholysis, splitting, and dystrophic \nchanges. Onycholysis may simulate onychomycosis; \ntherefore, fungal culture will be valuable in \ndiagnosis. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 26,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 354,
      "word_count": 49,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Intertriginous Psoriasis \nSebopsoriasis  \nThe skin fold areas are shades of red and orange, \nwith mild to severe itching. The characteristic sign \nis the uniform appearance (unlike tinea) and distinct \nborder (unlike candida). Generally, a complete skin \nexam will reveal other signs of psoriasis. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 27,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 299,
      "word_count": 43,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Psoriasis of the Scalp \nThe lesions are red, sharply defined plaques covered \nwith thick silvery scales. This distinguishes psoriasis \nfrom the diffuse or patchy redness and scaling of \nseborrheic dermatitis. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 28,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 210,
      "word_count": 30,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Pustular Psoriasis \nGenerally, a chronic, disabling condition of the \npalms and soles, it can also be a part of a very \nsevere generalized reaction. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 29,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 150,
      "word_count": 24,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Guttate Psoriasis \nA form of psoriasis characterized by the rapid \ndevelopment of myriad small lesions, 3 to 10 mm in \ndiameter, on all areas of the body, especially the \nextremities. More often seen in young people. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 30,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 218,
      "word_count": 36,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Herpes Simplex, Penis \nRed, sharply marginated, grouped vesicles usually \nbecome crusted sores within 48 hours. Typical \ndistribution includes prepuce, coronal sulcus, glans, \nshaft. Deep aching pain of the perineum may occur \n2 to 3 days before appearance of the skin lesions. \nItchy and painful, lesions generally recur in the \nsame location. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 31,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 346,
      "word_count": 51,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Herpes Simplex, Vulva \nPainful, recurrent, grouped vesicles. Viral \nshedding occurs even when no lesions are present. \nThis sexually transmitted disease can complicate \npregnancy. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 32,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 181,
      "word_count": 23,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Herpes Simplex, Perineum \nRecurrence of painful sores is a diagnostic sign. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 33,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 77,
      "word_count": 11,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Herpes Simplex in AIDS \nLesion in the perianal area becomes a deeply \nulcerated, very painful, disabling infection. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 34,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 117,
      "word_count": 17,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Condyloma Acuminatum \nGenital Warts  \nHighly contagious and sexually transmitted, soft, \nskin-colored, fleshy warts can be pin-head papules \nor cauliflower-like masses that are caused by the \nhuman papilloma virus. On the vulva, perianal area, \nvaginal walls, cervix, or on the shaft of the penis, \nwarts can be raised clusters and obviously wart-like, or so small they only \nbecome recognizable after application of 5% acetic acid (vinegar) for ten \nminutes. Lesions must be distinguished from condylomata lata caused by \nsyphilis. Diagnosis of syphilis is based on a positive serologic test or discovery \nof Treponema pallidum on darkfield examination. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 35,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 656,
      "word_count": 96,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Secondary Syphilis \nGeneralized maculopapular eruptions are most \ncommon, although lesions may be pustular or \nfollicular as well (or combinations of any of these \ntypes). Condylomata lata are raised, weeping \npapules on the moist areas of the skin and mucous \nmembranes. The patient generally feels sick, can \nhave regional lymphadenopathy, but complains only of minimal itching. \nDiagnosis of syphilis is based on a positive serologic test or discovery of \nTreponema pallidum on darkfield microscopy. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 36,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 504,
      "word_count": 73,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Scabies \nScabies is a common dermatitis caused by \ninfestation with Sarcoptes scabiei. The entire \nfamily may be affected. Skin lesions are scattered \ngroups of pruritic vesicles and pustules in \"runs\" or \n\"burrows\" on the sides of the fingers, palms, wrists, \nelbows, axillae, as well as around the waist and \ngroin. Itching occurs almost exclusively at night. \nMicroscopic examination of a scraping will reveal scabies mites, ova, and \nfeces. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 37,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 446,
      "word_count": 68,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Pediculosis \nLice \nPediculosis is a parasitic infestation of the skin of the \nscalp, trunk, or pubic areas. Itching may be very \nintense and scratching may result in deep \nexcoriations over the affected area. Head lice are \neasiest to see above the ears and at the nape of the neck. The nits (egg sacs) are \nattached to hairs, close to the skin. Body lice deposit visible nits on vellus hair. \nHead and body lice are similar in appearance and are 3 to 4 mm long. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 38,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 464,
      "word_count": 84,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Papular Urticaria \nAlmost exclusively in children, this is a widespread \nreaction to insect bites such as fleas, bedbugs, \nchiggers, or gnats, and may persist for long periods. \nThe tendency will fade with onset of adolescence. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 39,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 229,
      "word_count": 35,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Urticaria \nUsually intensely itching intradermal vascular \nreaction (wheals or hives). No epidermal changes \nsuch as scaling, papules, or blisters. More often has \nan unknown, nonspecific etiology, but can be \nrelated to medications, foods, and similar vascular­\nstimulating agents. Laboratory studies are not likely \nto be helpful in evaluation unless there are sugges­\ntive findings in the history and physical examination. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 40,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 427,
      "word_count": 60,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Molluscum Contagiosum \nCaused by a large pox virus, these smooth-walled, \ndome-shaped, pearly papules, 2 to \n5 mm in size, have an umbilicated center. \nOccasionally a significant inflammatory reaction \nwill occur. Principal sites are face, hands, lower \nabdomen, and genitals. A common viral infection \nseen in AIDS. It is more difficult to eradicate in \nthese patients. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 41,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 372,
      "word_count": 55,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Herpes Simplex \nSmall red-ringed blisters can occur anywhere, \nespecially around oral and genital areas. Associated \nand often preceded by burning and stinging. \nRegional lymph nodes may be swollen and tender. \nBlisters rupture early, leaving serous crusts which \ncan then become secondarily infected. Viral cultures \nand ELISA are positive. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 42,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 343,
      "word_count": 48,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Herpes Zoster \nRed-ringed blisters occur in a dermatomal \ndistribution of a nerve root. Papules change to \nvesicles which become pustules before crusting. \nNew lesions appear for up to one week. Regional \nlymph glands may be tender and swollen. Since this \nis primarily a nerve infection with secondary skin \nmanifestations, it is preceded, accompanied, and \nfollowed by pain. In elderly patients, it is often severe and prolonged. In \nimmunosuppressed patients, herpes zoster may disseminate, producing lesions \nbeyond the dermatome, visceral lesions, and encephalitis. Disseminated Zoster \nis a serious, sometimes life-threatening complication. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Handbook_of_Skin_Diseases.pdf",
      "page": 43,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 648,
      "word_count": 90,
      "title": "Handbook Of Skin Diseases",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
