    "text": "131\nFacial Swellings\nSection A\n■\n■\nSwab\nC&S – infection, e.g. erysipelas, insect bites.\nSPECIFIC INVESTIGATIONS\n■\n■\nHand X-ray\nAcromegaly.\n■\n■\nCT\nAcromegaly. Cushing’s disease. Fractures. Mediastinal tumours. \nCarcinoma of the air sinuses. Parotid tumours. Cavernous sinus \nthrombosis.\n■\n■\nSialography\nParotid conditions, e.g. stone in parotid duct.\n■\n■\nTFTs\nCretinism.\n■\n■\nPlasma cortisol\nCushing’s syndrome.\n■\n■\nGlucose tolerance test with growth hormone assay\nAcromegaly.\n!\n• Localised swelling with complaint of a blocked \nnose or a bloodstained nasal discharge is strongly \nsuggestive of carcinoma of the sinuses.\n• Parotid swelling with facial palsy suggests a \nmalignant parotid tumour with involvement of the \nfacial nerve.\n• Sudden generalised swelling of the face suggests an \nallergic reaction.\nSUBASH KC/NMC-15TH/2014\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Churchills_Pocketbook_of_Differential_Diagnosis (1).pdf",
      "page": 144,
      "chunk_index": 0,
