    "text": "211\nHeadache\nSection A\n■\n■\nTemporal artery biopsy\nInflammation and giant cells may be seen with temporal \narteritis. A normal biopsy does not, however, exclude the \ndisease, as there may be segmental involvement of the temporal \nartery.\n■\n■\nIntraocular pressure measurements\nTonometry will reveal high intraocular pressures with glaucoma.\n!\n• Although extremely common, headache should \nalways be investigated if there is any concern \nregarding sinister pathology, such as CVA or \nmalignancy.\n• Temporal arteritis must be treated immediately with \nhigh-dose corticosteroids to avoid complete and \nirreversible blindness.\nSUBASH KC/NMC-15TH/2014\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Churchills_Pocketbook_of_Differential_Diagnosis (1).pdf",
      "page": 224,
      "chunk_index": 0,
