    "text": "437\nThroat Conditions\nSection A\n■\n■\nSwab\nCulture and sensitivity – streptococcus and gonococcus.\n■\n■\nViral antibodies\nGlandular fever.\n■\n■\nECG\nCardiac ischaemia.\n■\n■\nCXR\nMetastases.\nSPECIFIC INVESTIGATIONS\n■\n■\nIndirect laryngoscopy\nInfection. Malignancy.\n■\n■\nOGD\nOesophagitis. Hiatus hernia.\n■\n■\nHIV testing\nAIDS.\n■\n■\nBiopsy\nBenign versus malignant.\n■\n■\nCT\nTumour. Spread of tumour.\n!\n• Throat swabs are useful only in the management  \nof recurrent or persistent cases.\n• Occasionally a sore throat may be the first \nsymptom of a more serious disorder, e.g. diabetes, \nagranulocytosis or malignancy.\n• Prolonged and persistent sore throat in an elderly \npatient is a cause for concern as it may be an \nindication of malignancy.\nSUBASH KC/NMC-15TH/2014\n",
