  {
    "text": "466\nVaginal Discharge\nGENERAL INVESTIGATIONS\n■\n■\nGram stain\nGram stain on cervical or urethral exudate – Gram-negative \nintracellular diplococci in gonorrhoea.\n■\n■\nVaginal pH testing\npH>4.5 in bacterial vaginosis. pH<4.5 in candidiasis.\n■\n■\nTriple swabs\nHigh vaginal swab – bacterial vaginosis, candidiasis, trichomonal \nvaginalis.\nEndocervical swab – gonorrhoea.\nEndocervical swab – Chlamydia DNA amplification for Chlamydia \ntrachomatis.\n■\n■\nUrine analysis (first catch)\nDNA amplification for Chlamydia.\nSPECIFIC INVESTIGATIONS\n■\n■\nHysteroscopy\nMalignancy/menorrhagia.\n■\n■\nD&C\nMalignancy.\n■\n■\nBarium enema\nColovaginal fistula.\n■\n■\nMRI\nColovaginal fistula.\n!\n• Malignancy is a likely cause of vaginal discharge in \npost-menopausal years. Always perform a full pelvic \nexamination.\n• Always take a full sexual history and, if sexually \ntransmitted disease is suspected, refer to the GUM \nclinic for full assessment and contact tracing.\n• Vaginal discharge is rare before puberty. Consider \nforeign body or sexual abuse.\n• Recurrent thrush may be a presenting symptom of \ndiabetes mellitus.\nSUBASH KC/NMC-15TH/2014\n",
