  {
    "text": "  \nHead injury: introduction\n363\nIndications for referral to hospital\nAny one of the following criteria indicates the need for hospital assessment:\n\t•\t Impaired conscious level at any time.\n\t•\t Amnesia for the incident or subsequent events.\n\t•\t Neurological symptoms (vomiting, severe and persistent headache, \nseizures).\n\t•\t Clinical evidence of a skull fracture (CSF leak, peri-​orbital haematoma).\n\t•\t Signiﬁcant extracranial injuries.\n\t•\t Worrying mechanism (high-​energy, possible NAI), possible penetrating \ninjury).\n\t•\t Continuing uncertainty about the diagnosis after the ﬁrst assessment.\n\t•\t Medical comorbidity (anticoagulant use, alcohol abuse).\n\t•\t Adverse social factors (eg alone at home).\nThe following are highly recommended:\n\t•\t The SIGN guideline on head injury is accessible at M https://​www.sign.\nac.uk\n\t•\t The NICE clinical guidelines on head injury updated in 2019 are \naccessible at M https://​www.nice.org.uk\nIntracranial pressure\nIntracranial blood volume\nFig. 8.10  ICP according to intracranial blood volume.\n \n",
