    "text": "560\n560\nChapter 11  Ophthalmology\nPupillary abnormalities\nPupillary examination may yield valuable information (see Table 11.1).\nTable 11.1  Syndromes relating to examination of pupils\n \nDescription\nCommon causes\nAbducens nerve palsy\nDiplopia, inability to \nlook outwards\nTrauma, tumour, \nWernicke’s syndrome\nAdie pupil\nPupil is dilated and \nresponds abnormally \nslowly to light\nCommonly part of \nHolmes-​Adie syndrome—​\ncause unclear\nAnisocoria\nUnequal pupils\nMay be physiological or \nreflect disease process \n(eg third nerve palsy or \nHorner’s syndrome)\nArgyll Robertson pupil \n(usually bilateral)\nSmall pupils that \naccommodate, but do not \nconstrict, to light\nPathognomonic of tertiary \nsyphilis\nHorner’s syndrome \n(sympathetic nerve \ndamage)\nMiosis, ptosis, and \nanhidrosis (usually \nunilateral)\nLung cancer, trauma, lateral \nmedullary syndrome, \ncarotid artery dissection\nHutchinson’s pupil\n(Ipsilateral) pupil dilates \nand is unreactive to light\nCompression of the \noculomotor nerve can \nfollow trauma or tumour \noften due to temporal lobe \nherniation\nMarcus Gunn pupil \n(relative afferent \npupillary defect)\nLight shone in affected \neye causes slow direct and \nconsensual pupil reactions \n(light in normal eye gives \nbrisk direct and consensual \nreactions)\nOptic neuritis, giant cell \narteritis, optic nerve \ntrauma, retinal detachment\nMiosis\nExcessive pupillary \nconstriction\nDrugs such as opioids \n(bilateral miosis), Horner’s \nsyndrome (unilateral \nmiosis)\nMydriasis\nExcessive pupillary \ndilatation\nDrugs, post-​cardiac arrest\nOculomotor nerve palsy\nPtosis, mydriasis, and \nmovement of the eyeball \ndownward and outward \n(unopposed action of \nfourth and sixth nerves)\nTrauma, tumour, \naneurysm, demyelinating \ndisorders, cavernous sinus \nthrombosis\nTrochlear nerve palsy\nVertical diplopia; \ninability to look down \nand in\nTrauma, haemorrhage, \ninfarction\n \n",
