    "text": "594\n594\nChapter 13  Obstetrics and gynaecology\nPrescribing in pregnancy\nConsult the BNF before prescribing drugs in pregnancy or during breast­\nfeeding. The following are generally considered safe in pregnancy: penicillin, \ncephalosporins, nystatin, paracetamol, chlorphenamine, and cimetidine.\nAvoid: trimethoprim, tetracyclines, streptomycin, warfarin, thiazides, and \nsodium valproate.\n36 weeks\n40 weeks\n22 weeks\n16 weeks\n12 weeks\nFig. 13.2  Uterine size in pregnancy.\nTable 13.1  Normal values in pregnant and non-​pregnant women\nValue\nNon-​pregnant\nPregnant\nHaematocrit\n0.37–​0.47\n0.32–​0.41\nHaemoglobin (g/​L)\n115–​160\n110–​150\nWCC (/​L)\n4.0–​11.0 × 109\n5.0–​16.0 × 109\nPlatelets (/​L)\n150–​400 × 109\n134–​400 × 109\nESR (mm/​hr)\n(age in years + 10)/​2\n44–​114\nFibrinogen (g/​L)\n2–​4\n4–​6\nAlbumin (g/​L)\n35–​50\n28–​40\nUrea (mmol/​L)\n2.5–​6.7\n1.6–​6.0\nCreatinine (mmol/​L)\n<110\n38–​90\npCO2 (kPa)\n4.5–​6.0 (34–​46mmHg)\n3.6–​4.2 (27–​32mmHg)\npO2 (kPa)\n>10.6 (>80.6mmHg)\n>10.6 (>80.6mmHg)\nHCO3\n− (mmol/​L)\n24–​28\n18–​23\n \n",
