  {
    "text": "  \nAnkle and foot injuries in children\n755\nAnkle and foot injuries in children\nAnkle injuries\nAnkle ligament injuries are less common in children than in adults, partly \nreflecting the fact that ligaments are often stronger than the bone to which \nthey attach. The presence of epiphyses can make it difficult to establish \nwhether or not a fracture is present on X-​rays. If there is no obvious frac-\nture on X-​ray, but there is much tenderness/​swelling over the distal tibial or \nﬁbular epiphysis, treat it as a growth plate injury (undisplaced Salter–​Harris \ntype I fracture) with BKPOP, crutches, elevation, analgesia, and follow-​up \nin the ED or fracture clinic. Some fractures (see, for example, Fig. 15.38) \nrequire admission for manipulation under GA.\nFoot injuries\nA wide range of foot injuries occur in children. A  common difficulty is \ndistinguishing between the normal apophysis at the base of the fifth MT \n(see Fig. 15.39) and a fracture. The normal apophysis is typically longitu-\ndinally parallel to the fifth MT (as opposed to a fracture which is typically \ntransverse or oblique—​as shown in Fig. 9.7).\nCalcaneal injuries\n(See % Foot fractures and dislocations, pp. 504–5.)\nFig. 15.38  Ankle \nfracture in a 12y old.\nFig. 15.39  Normal foot in an 11y old.\n \n \n \n \n",
