    "text": "Page 37 of 45\n\nJunctional Arrhythmias\n\n\" The atria and sinus node do not perform their normal pacemaking functions.\n® A junctional escape rhythm begins.\n\nTisttt\n\nes Seats ESE! Sass Gates S2ss3 Estes Sees tad Geese teas cosas Cgtes toaed Osees S809 cose: tases gaa; bases tpeai Susad\nInverted P wave -Absent!P wave. $5353 (23g) a SE\n\nae\n\n—\n\n;\n'\n;\n;\n;\n;\n\nRate: 40—60 bpm\n\nRhythm: Regular\n\nP Waves: Absent, inverted, buried, or retrograde\nPR Interval: None, short, or retrograde\n\nQRS: Normal (0.06-—0.10 sec)\n\n¥ Clinical Tip: Sinus node disease that causes inappropriate sinus node slowing may exacerbate this rhythm.\nYoung, healthy adults, especially those with increased vagal tone during sleep, often have periods of junctional\nrhythm that is completely benign, not requiring intervention.\n\nVentricular Arrhythmias\n\nIn all ventricular rhythms, the QRS complex is >0.10 sec. P Waves are absent or, if visible, have no\nconsistent relationship to the QRS complex.\n\nRate: 20-40 bpm\n\nRhythm: Regular\n\nP Waves: None\n\nPR Interval: None\n\nQRS: Wide (>0.10 sec), bizarre appearance\n\nY Clinical Tip: Diminished cardiac output is expected because of the slow heart rate. An idioventricular\nrhythm may be called an agonal rhythm when the heart rate drops below 20 bpm. An agonal rhythm is gener-\nally terminal and is usually the last rhythm before asystole.\n\n",
