    "text": "Page 40 of 45\n\n® In monomorphic VT, QRS complexes have the same shape and amplitude.\n\nRate: 100-250 bpm\n\nRhythm: Regular\n\nP Waves: None or not associated with the QRS\nPR Interval: None\n\nQRS: Wide (>0.10 sec), bizarre appearance\n\nY Clinical Tip: It is important to confirm the presence or absence of pulses because monomorphic VT may\nbe perfusing or nonperfusing.\n\nY Clinical Tip: Monomorphic VT will probably deteriorate into VF or unstable VT if sustained and not treated.\n\n® In polymorphic VT, ORS complexes vary in shape and amplitude.\n© The OT interval is normal or long. fB.com/medicalonlinel\n\nRate: 100-250 bpm\n\nRhythm: Regular or irregular\n\nP Waves: None or not associated with the ORS\nPR Interval: None\n\nORS: Wide (>0.10 sec), bizarre appearance\n\n¥ Clinical Tip: It is important to determine whether pulses are present because polymorphic VT may be\nperfusing or nonperfusing.\n\nY Clinical Tip: Consider electrolyte abnormalities as a possible cause.\n",
