  {
    "text": "evidence; Analysis 1.2). • GLP-1 receptor agonists may have little or no efect on the risk of cardiovascular death compared to placebo (RR 0.84, 95% CI 0.68 to 1.05; I2 = 42%; 7 studies, 17,801 participants; low-certainty evidence; Analysis 1.3) in people with all CKD stages and type 2 diabetes. Similar results were seen for the subgroups CKD stages 1-2 (Analysis 1.3.1) and CKD stages 3-5 (Analysis 1.3.2). The results of the meta-analysis of HR in trials are shown in Table 1 (HR 0.90, 95% CI 0.73 to 1.12; I2 = 54%; 3 studies; low-certainty evidence; Analysis 1.4). Major adverse cardiovascular events 3-point MACE was defined in all included studies as the composite cardiovascular outcome, including cardiovascular death, nonfatal MI, or nonfatal stroke. • GLP-1 receptor agonists probably decreased 3-point MACE compared to placebo (RR 0.84, 95% CI 0.73 to 0.98; I2 = 65%; 4 studies, 19,825 participants; moderate-certainty evidence; Analysis 1.5) in people with all CKD stages and type 2 diabetes.\nSimilar results were seen for the subgroups CKD stages 1-2\n(Analysis 1.5.1) and CKD stages 3-5 (Analysis 1.5.2). The meta-\nanalysis based on HR values has been reported in Table 1 (HR\n0.80, 95% CI 0.73 to 0.89; I2 = 53%; 7 studies; moderate-certainty\nevidence; Analysis 1.6).\n4-point MACE was defined as the composite cardiovascular\noutcome including cardiovascular death, nonfatal MI, nonfatal\nstroke, plus coronary revascularisation or hospitalisation for\nunstable angina pectoris or heart failure.\n•\nGLP-1 receptor agonists probably decreased 4-point MACE\ncompared to placebo (RR 0.77, 95% CI 0.67 to 0.89; 1 study,\n2,158 participants; moderate-certainty evidence; Analysis 1.7)\nin people with CKD stages 3-5 and type 2 diabetes. The meta-\nanalysis based on HR values has been reported in Table 1 (HR\n0.86, 95% CI 0.74 to 1.01; I2 = 58%; 2 studies; moderate-certainty\nevidence; Analysis 1.8).\nKidney failure\nGLP-1 receptor agonists probably had little or no efect on kidney\nfailure, defined as the need for KRT, compared to placebo (RR 0.86,\n95% CI 0.66 to 1.13; I2 = 0%; 3 studies, 4,134 participants; moderate-\ncertainty evidence; Analysis 1.9) in people with all CKD stages and\ntype 2 diabetes. Similar results were seen for the subgroup CKD\nstages 3-5 (Analysis 1.9.2).\nThe meta-analysis based on HR values has been reported in Table 1\n(HR 0.84, 95% CI 0.63 to 1.12; 1 study; moderate-certainty evidence;\nAnalysis 1.10).\nGlucagon-like peptide 1 (GLP-1) receptor agonists for people with chronic kidney disease and diabetes (Review)\nCopyright © 2025 The Cochrane Collaboration. Published by John Wiley & Sons, Ltd.\n14\n",
