      "year": "2023",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "c) Combined neutropenia and lyphocytopenia\nQ Marrow infiltration (vide supra)\n4 Drug-induced (cytotoxics)\n\nAplastic marrow (idiopathic, benzene, irradiation)\n\n2) Leucocytosis\na) Neutrophilia\nJ Inflammation\ne Infective — bacterial\n¢ Noninfective\n- Tissue necrosis / Infarction / Trauma of burns\n- Autoimmune disorders: SLE/vasculitis\n- Gout\n1 Neoplasia\ne Myeloproliferative neoplasms, other malignancies\n4 Drugs (glucocorticoids, lithium)\nAcute stress or haemorrhage\n\nO\n\n1 Post-splenectomy\n\n9 Leukaemoid reactions (white cell count (WCC) greater than\n50.0 x 10°/L — normal range 4.0 — 11.0 x 10°/L)\n\ne Severe sepsis\n- Acute (septicaemia)\n- Chronic (TB)\ne Malignancy\ne Severe haemolysis\ne (Chronic myeloid leukaemia is a myeloproliferative disorder not a\nleukaemoid reaction)\nb) Lymphocytosis\nInfections (viral, TB, pertussis, other)\n9 Acute and chronic lymphocytic leukaemia\n(4 Other (adrenal insufficiency)\n\nKey Objective\n¢ Interpret the clinical setting in which the leucocyte abnormality occurs as\n\nthis will often suggest the correct diagnosis and direct further investigation.\n\n",
