      "char_count": 912,
      "word_count": 123,
      "title": "AMC Anthology of Medical Conditions",
      "author": "Australian Medical Council",
      "year": "2023",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "023 Cyanosis/Hypoxaemia / Hypoxia\n\n023A Cyanosis / Hypoxia / Apnoea in Children\n\nOverview\n\nEvaluation of cyanosis and hypoxia in children depends heavily on the age of the child.\nCyanosis is an ominous finding, especially in the older child, and differentiation between\nperipheral and central is essential in order to mount appropriate management.\n\nCauses of cyanosis or hypoxaemia\n1) Neonatal\n\na) Central\n(4 Cyanotic congenital heart disease\n\n* Increased pulmonary blood flow (transposition, truncus arteriosus,\ntotal anomalous pulmonary venous return, hypoplastic left heart)\n\n* Obstruction to pulmonary blood flow (tricuspid, pulmonary atresia)\n(J Respiratory insufficiency\n\ne Pulmonary (respiratory distress syndrome, sepsis, aspiration,\ndiaphragmatic hernia)\n\n¢ Central nervous system (CNS) (maternal sedative, asphyxia,\nintracranial haemorrhage, hypoglycaemia)\n\nb) Peripheral vascular (‘physiologic acrocyanosis’, sepsis, cardiogenic/\nseptic shock, thrombosis, vasomotor instability)\n\n2) =Infant and child\n\na) Central\n4) Decreased oxygenation of haemoglobin\n\n* Respiratory (pneumonia, cystic fibrosis, embolus, aspiration /\nforeign body, CNS depression)\n\ne Cardiac disease — cyanotic congenital heart disease, severe\ncongestive cardiac failure (CCF) from any cause\n\nAbnormalities of haemoglobin (methaemoglobinaemia)\nb) Peripheral\n4 Vascular problem (Raynaud disease, sepsis)\n\nObstruction (superior vena cava syndrome, deep venous thrombosis\n(DVT))\n\nQ) Hyperviscosity (polycythaemia)\n\n123\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "AMC Anthology of Medical Conditions.pdf",
      "page": 97,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 1495,
      "word_count": 190,
      "title": "AMC Anthology of Medical Conditions",
