      "word_count": 202,
      "title": "Talley & Connor's Clinical Examination",
      "author": "Talley & Connor",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Figure 4.9 Finger clubbing: (a) appearance; (b) phalangeal depth ratio\n \nBefore leaving the nails, look for splinter haemorrhages in the nail beds\n(Figure 4.10). These are linear haemorrhages lying parallel to the long axis of\nthe nail. They are most often due to trauma, particularly in manual workers.\nHowever, an important cause is infective endocarditis (page 79), which is a\nbacterial or other infection of the heart valves or part of the endocardium. In\nthis disease splinter haemorrhages are probably the result of a vasculitis in the\nnail bed, but this is controversial. Other rare causes of splinter haemorrhages\ninclude vasculitis, as in rheumatoid arthritis, polyarteritis nodosa or the\nantiphospholipid syndrome, sepsis elsewhere in the body, haematological\nmalignancy or profound anaemia.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Clinical Examination-Talley & Connor-pdf.pdf",
      "page": 130,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
