    }
  },
  {
    "text": "5. Alcohol-associated parotitis\n6. Malnutrition\n7. Severe dehydration: as occurs in renal failure, terminal\ncarcinomatosis and severe infections\nUnilateral\n1. Mixed parotid tumour (occasionally bilateral)\n2. Tumour infiltration, which usually causes painless unilateral\nenlargement and may cause facial nerve palsy\n3. Duct blockage, e.g. salivary calculus\n \n* Johann von Mikulicz-Radecki (1850–1905), professor of surgery, Breslau. He described this condition in 1892.\nSubmandibular gland enlargement is most often due to a calculus. This\nmay be palpable bimanually (Figure 6.15). The examiner’s gloved index\nfinger is placed on the floor of the mouth beside the tongue, feeling between\nit and fingers placed behind the body of the mandible. It may also be\nenlarged in chronic liver disease.\nFigure 6.15 Examination of the submandibular gland\n \nThe mouth\n",
