      "word_count": 166,
      "title": "Talley & Connor's Clinical Examination",
      "author": "Talley & Connor",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "o e\ne y to be a ected.\nco\nect ve t ssue d seases, espec a y\nscleroderma, Raynaud’s phenomenon can occur and may lead to the\nformation of digital ulcers (Table 9.7).\nTABLE 9.7 Causes of Raynaud’s phenomenon (white-blue-red fingers and toes in response\nto cold)\n \n \n \n \n \n \nReflex\nRaynaud’s disease (idiopathic)\nVibrating machinery injury\nCervical spondylosis\nConnective tissue disease\nScleroderma, diffuse or limited type\nMixed connective tissue disease\nSystemic lupus erythematosus\nPolyarteritis nodosa\nRheumatoid arthritis\nPolymyositis\nVasculitis\nArterial disease\nEmbolism or thrombosis\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Clinical Examination-Talley & Connor-pdf.pdf",
      "page": 575,
