  },
  {
    "text": "certain distributions suggest specific diagnoses. Third, the pattern of the\nlesions—such as linear, annular (ring-shaped), reticulated (net-like),\nserpiginous (snake-like) or grouped—also helps establish the diagnosis. Then\npalpate the lesions, noting consistency, tenderness, temperature, depth and\nmobility. Types of skin lesions are shown in Figure 15.4 and a clinical\nalgorithm for diagnosis is presented in Figure 15.5.\nTABLE 15.3 Dermatological terms\n \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "Clinical Examination-Talley & Connor-pdf.pdf",
