    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Look in the fundi for cottonwool spots (common in AIDS patients),\nscars (e.g. toxoplasmosis—Figure 16.5) or retinitis (e.g. cytomegalovirus-\ninduced retinitis with perivascular haemorrhages and fluffy exudates, which\ncan cause blindness of rapid onset—Figure 16.6).10 There may be signs of\ndementia (AIDS encephalopathy).\nFigure 16.5 Retinal toxoplasmosis—old chorioretinal scar\n \nFigure 16.6 Cytomegalovirus retinitis\n \nReferences\n \n1. Knockaert DC, Vanneste LJ, Vanneste SB, Bobbaers HJ. Fever of unknown origin in the\n1980s. An update of the diagnostic spectrum. Arch Intern Med. 1992;152:51-55.\n2. Whitby M. The febrile patient. Aust Fam Phys. 1993;22:1753-1755. 1758–1761\n3. Arbo M, et al. Fever of nosocomial origin: etiology, risk factors and outcomes. Am J\nMed. 1993;95:505-515.\n4 Mourad O Palda V DetskyAS Acomprehensive evidence based approachto fever of\n",
