  {
    "text": "'all-things-considered' decisions. The dominant ethical principles applying to the practice of medicine in Australia at present reflect a combination of historical influences dating back to the time of Hippocrates as well as the Judeo-Christian thinking which the first Europeans brought with them a little over two hundred years ago. The values of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples have not yet been fully recognised by non-indigenous Australians although guidelines developed recently for the purpose of considering health research involving Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples provide considerable relevant insights into the values and ethical views of these Australians1. Views on ethics may change over time and are context, time and culture specific. A society's jointly held view on ethical issues will also influence personal values and views of ethical matters, especially during a person's formative years. Laws are specific, ethics are conceptual. For doctors in Australia, several ethical codes of practice exist. The most widely known code is that of the Australian Medical Association\n2, \n                                              \n1 Values and Ethics. Guidelines for ethical conduct in Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander research. NHMRC. \nCanberra, 2003. http://www.nhmrc.gov.au \n2 Australian Medical Association Code of Ethics 2003, http://www.ama.com.au \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "AMC Handbook of Clinical Assessment.pdf",
      "page": 610,
      "chunk_index": 1,
      "total_chunks": 2,
