    "text": "CHAPTER 8  A summary of the cardiovascular examination\t\n155\n (From Baker T, Nikolić G, O’Connor S. Practical cardiology, 2nd edn. \n©2008, Sydney: Elsevier Australia.)\nNormal long-axis M-mode measurements. \nFIGURE 8.12\nBoth the fractional shortening (change in the \ndimensions between systole and diastole; \nnormally >27%) and the ejection fraction \nhave been calculated\n(From Baker T, Nikolić G, O’Connor S. Practical cardiology, 2nd edn. \n©2008, Sydney: Elsevier Australia.)\nM-mode measurements in a patient with \nhypertension. \nFIGURE 8.14\nThere is symmetrical thickening of the left \nventricular wall\n(From Baker T, Nikolić G, O’Connor S. Practical cardiology, 2nd edn. \n©2008, Sydney: Elsevier Australia.)\nThe left ventricle is very dilated. The end-\ndiastolic dimension is 70 mm (<57). The \nfractional shortening is only 14% (>25–27). \nContraction of the septum and posterior wall \nare equally reduced, typical of dilated \ncardiomyopathy (p 139)\nFIGURE 8.13\nM-mode echo from a 34-year-old man with 2 \nmonths of increasing dyspnoea, diagnosed \ninitially as asthma.\n(From Baker T, Nikolić G, O’Connor S. Practical cardiology, 2nd edn. \n©2008, Sydney: Elsevier Australia.)\nNormal long-axis view of the heart. \nFIGURE 8.15\nThe mitral valve leaflets can be seen wide \nopen in this diastolic frame. The apex of the \nheart is on the left and the right ventricle is at \nthe top of the picture\n",
