    "text": "CHAPTER 8  A summary of the cardiovascular examination\t\n159\nOSCE REVISION TOPICS\n1.\t This woman has been breathless. Please assess her \nchest X-ray. (p 147)\n2.\t Look at this normal chest X-ray and point out the \ncardiac borders and the positions of the cardiac \nchambers. Indicate where the aortic and mitral valve \nlie. (p 152)\n3.\t Perform a standard cardiovascular examination on \nthis patient. Explain what you are doing as you go \nalong. (p 146)\nT&O’C ESSENTIALS\n1.\t Chest X-ray, ECG and echocardiography are \nextensions of the cardiovascular examination.\n2.\t ECGs and echocardiograms lead to no radiation \nexposure and can be repeated as required.\n3.\t Students need to have an approach to \ninterpreting chest X-rays and ECGs and an \nunderstanding of the information that can be \nobtained from an echocardiogram.\n4.\t The initial diagnosis of myocardial infarction \nshould be based on the history, physical \nexamination and ECG and should not be \ndelayed until blood test results are to hand.\n",
