    "text": "390\t\nSECTION 7  The rheumatological system\nThe pain may have begun suddenly, suggesting a disc \nprolapse, or more gradually owing to disc degeneration.\nPostural tendon and muscle strains are common \ncauses of temporary neck pain. These are often related \nto overuse. Ask about the patient’s occupation and \nwhether work or recreational activities involve repeated \nand prolonged extension of the neck (e.g. painters and \ncyclists). These patients often describe neck stiffness, \nPosterior arch\nof atlas (C1)\nSpinous process of axis (C2)\nVertebra prominens\n(spinous process of C7)\nTransverse process\nof first thoracic\nvertebra T1\nPedicle of T4\nArticular surface\nfor tubercle\nof sixth rib\nTransverse\nprocess of T7\nTransverse process\nof first lumbar\nvertebra (L1) \nSpinous\nprocess of T9\nSpinous\nprocess of L1\nSuperior articular\nprocess of L3\nSuperior articular\nprocess of sacrum\nInferior articular\nprocess of L3\nMedian sacral\ncrest\nSacral\ntuberosity\nCoccyx\nAuricular\nsurface of sacrum\nSacral promontory\nL5 Vertebra\nSuperior vertebral\nnotch of T10\nInferior vertebral\nnotch of T9\nIntervertebral foramen\nIntervertebral\ndisc T3–4 \nDemi-facet for\nsecond rib on T2\nFacet for head\nof first rib\nTransverse\nprocess of\nthird cervical\nvertebra C3 \nTip of Dens\nof axis\na\nb\nFIGURE 24.24 \n(a) Structure of the spine; (b) MRI scan of the lumbar spine showing the anatomical features \nseen in (a) \n(MRI scan courtesy of M Thomson, National Capital Diagnostic Imaging, Canberra.)\nand pain and muscle spasm are often present. The \nrepeated holding of a telephone between the shoulder \nand the ear can cause nerve root problems. Neck \nmovement may cause radicular symptoms such as \nparaesthesias in the distribution of a cervical nerve \nafter a hyperextension injury or cervical spine arthritis. \nAsk about paraesthesias and weakness in the arms \nand hands.\n",
