    "text": "\t\nCHAPTER 29  Correlation of physical signs and endocrine disease\t\n465\nTEXT BOX 29.1 \nA summary of the examination of the patient with diabetes mellitus\nLying\n1.\t General inspection\nWeight—obesity\nHydration\nEndocrine facies\nPigmentation—haemochromatosis, etc.\n2.\t Legs\nInspect\n○\t Skin—necrobiosis, hair loss, infection, \npigmented scars, atrophy, ulceration, \ninjection sites\n○\t Muscle wasting\nPalpate\n○\t Temperature of feet (cold, blue due to \n‘small’ or ‘large’ vessel disease, warm due \nto acute Charcot’s joint)\n○\t Peripheral pulses\nFemoral (auscultate)\nPopliteal\nPosterior tibial\nDorsalis pedis\n3.\t Arms\nInspect\n○\t Skin lesions (skin tags, acanthosis)\nPulse\n4.\t Eyes\nFundi—cataracts, rubeosis, retinal disease\nIII nerve palsy, other cranial nerves\n5.\t Mouth\nMonilia\nInfection\n6.\t Neck\nCarotid arteries—palpate, auscultate\n7.\t Chest\nSigns of infection\n8.\t Abdomen\nLiver—fat infiltration; rarely haemochromatosis\nInjection sites\nNeurological assessment\n○\t Femoral nerve mononeuritis\n○\t Peripheral neuropathy\n9.\t Other\nUrine analysis—glycosuria, ketones, proteinuria\nBlood pressure—lying and standing\nOedema\nAcetone on breath\nDiabetes mellitus \nFIGURE 29.1 \n",
