    "text": "612\t\nSECTION 9  The nervous system\nc\nb\na\nThese CT scans without contrast injection ((a) and (b)) show low-density changes confined to \nthe deep subcortical regions in a patient with acute left-sided weakness. The image in (c) \ntaken 2 days later shows extensive haemorrhage within the area of infarction, which has \nruptured into the ventricular system \n(From Haaga J. CT and MRI of the whole body, 5th edn. St Louis, MO: Mosby, 2008.)\nFIGURE 36.4 \nHaemorrhagic stroke.  \na\nb\nThe lesions of multiple sclerosis on MRI tend to involve the periventricular area, the corpus \ncallosum and the optic nerves (solid white arrows). (a) The lesions produce discrete, globular \nfoci of high-signal intensity (white) on T2-weighted MRI scans. (b) Ovoid lesions with their long \naxis perpendicular to the ventricular surface are called Dawson’s fingers (solid white arrows) \n(From Herring W. Learning radiology: recognising the basics, 2nd edn. Philadelphia: Saunders, 2011.)\nFIGURE 36.5 \nMultiple sclerosis. \n",
