    "text": "Foreword by Sarah Dalton\nThe more things change, the more they stay the same. \nWe live in a world where healthcare stands to be \nsignificantly disrupted by artificial intelligence, machine \nlearning and big data but at the same time as doctors \nwe have a fundamental responsibility to connect with \nour patients, to hear their stories and to diagnose and \ntreat their conditions with wisdom and compassion. \nSo, no matter how precise medicine can become, the \nfoundations of taking a history and performing a \ncomprehensive physical examination have never been \nmore important.\nClinical Examination by Talley and O’Connor has \nbeen widely recognised as providing the cornerstone \nto learning physical diagnoses over many decades. It \nis with some pride that I have kept my original first \nedition that accompanied me throughout medical \nschool all those years ago. The words of wisdom then \nare just as true today—that ‘treatment of the patient \nbegins the moment one reaches the bedside’. But even \nmore true, at this time heralded by over-diagnosis and \nover-investigation in healthcare, is the need to use the \nsimplest, most effective and arguably most reliable of all \ninvestigative approaches—a good history and physical \nexamination.\nIn this most recent iteration, the authors have \nexpanded their repertoire and turned their tried and \ntested formula to all medical specialties, ranging from \npaediatric medicine to specific approaches to ears, eyes, \nnose and throat assessments. The companion series \noffers the same structured approach to diagnoses, \nhighlighting important differences and bringing a \nwealth of information destined to prepare any candidate \nadmirably for a short or long case in these areas. The \ntext outlines tips and tricks designed to demystify \nthe sometimes challenging task of assessing neonates \nand children, discussing a range of approaches from \nmaternal health assessment to talking to adolescents \nand their families.\nMost doctors care for young people at some point \nin their career, and young people with medical \nconditions most definitely grow up. Thankfully our \npatients are living longer and living better, but caring \nfor chronic and complex conditions means we all need \nsome understanding of specialty issues. Many doctors \ndo not feel particularly well prepared for this challenge, \nmaking this text relevant to all clinicians. Whilst there \nis much we do not know about the future of medicine, \none thing we know for certain is that healthy young \npeople create healthy futures. From little things, big \nthings grow.\nDr Sarah Dalton \nBMed MAppMgt(Hlth) FRACP\nPaediatric Emergency Physician, \nThe Children’s Hospital at Westmead\nPresident, Division of Paediatrics and Child Health, \nThe Royal Australasian College of Physicians\n",
