    "text": "Foreword by David Ellwood\nThe clinical practice of Obstetrics & Gynaecology \n(O&G) presents medical students with challenges and \nopportunities. It is, by its very nature, a very practical \ndiscipline of medicine and for this reason many students \nwill enjoy their clinical attachments immensely. O&G \nhas enormous breadth and depth and to understand \nmany common presentations requires a detailed \nknowledge of pelvic anatomy, reproductive physiology, \nmicrobiology and psychology, as well as many other \nbranches of clinical medicine. But the standard \napproaches to the patient of history and examination \nbring with them some unique difficulties in terms of \nclinical skills development. Virtually all examinations \nare intimate, and history taking must embrace the fact \nthat there are significant psychosocial aspects of both \ngynaecological and obstetric problems.\nThis second volume of the well-known Talley & \nO’Connor’s Clinical Examination (8th Edition) contains \nseparate chapters on gynaecological and obstetric history \nand examination. They can both be read separately as \nstand-alone aids for students, but will also complement \neach other when read together as there are many areas \nof overlap. Both are clear, concise, and contain many \npractical tips which will be valuable for both the junior \nmedical student encountering these clinical experiences \nfor the first time, as well as the more senior student \nrevising for final examinations. The frameworks for \nhistory taking and examination, and the many clear \nillustrations, go a long way to demystifying the subject \nmatter.\nIt is especially important for the medical student to \nunderstand some of the complexities of O&G practice. \nThe clinical etiquette around intimate gynaecological \nand obstetric examination is extremely important, \nincluding the use of chaperones. Understanding the \ninterface between a woman’s gynaecological and \nsexual health wellbeing, and how this is impacted by \npsychological factors, is also vital. In modern O&G, \nmuch of the information that used to be derived from \nclinical examination is now obtained by the liberal use \nof ultrasound, but there are many times when both \nare essential and an appreciation of how this works in \npractice is essential. These chapters manage to provide \nclear and practical advice across all of these areas.\nFinally, the diligent student of medicine will know \nthat volumes such as this, important though they are, \nare only companions to learning, and it is the patient \nfrom whom most can be learned. Armed with this \npractical guide, students should be very well-equipped \nto take advantage of all of their clinical learning \nopportunities, which will ultimately be for the good \nof the women who they encounter in the future as \npatients.\nDavid Ellwood\nProfessor of Obstetrics and Gynaecology\n",
