    "text": "child, either sitting in the parent’s lap or playing on the floor. Assess the child’s state of wellbeing (does the child look sick or well, under- or overnourished? How active is he or she?). Note any asymmetry of movement or abnormal posturing, and evaluate the child’s skin colour and breathing pattern. Observe the way the child interacts with the parents and with other people. Take time to allow the child to become acquainted and comfortable with you. As mentioned, much of the examination can be performed with the child in the parent’s lap—usually the place where the child is likely to feel the most safe. Be prepared to play with, be silly with and interact with the child in a friendly, calm and quiet manner wherever possible. Some age-appropriate toys (well cleaned between patients) can be very helpful; when all else fails, using a bubble wand to blow a few well-timed bubbles should soothe and win over even \nthe most upset child. Never forget to warm your hands \nbefore touching a child.\nWhereas the young child may be most comfortable \non the parent’s lap, slightly older children usually sit \nb\t This means an infection acquired when a person is under medical \ncare—usually in hospital.\n",
