    "text": "634\t\nSECTION 10  PAEDIATRIC AND NEONATAL HISTORY AND EXAMINATION \n\t\nAcromion\nOlecranon\n40% of\ncircumference\nat midpoint\nCubital fossa\nAcromion\nOlecranon\na\nb\nc\n(a, b) Determining the proper cuff size. \n(c) Measuring the blood pressure \n(From Hockenberry MJ, Wilson D. Wong’s essentials of pediatric nursing, \n8th edn. St Louis, MO: Mosby, 2009.)\nFIGURE 37.3 \npopulation data tables—that are age, sex and sometimes \nheight matched—in order to diagnose hypertension \naccurately.11\nHypotension is also uncommon in children, but it \nwarrants rapid evaluation to exclude hypovolaemia or \nsepsis, which can lead to shock (circulatory failure; see \nTable 37.2). Remember, hypotension is a late sign in \ncritically ill children with other signs often present \nprior to hypotension (e.g. tachycardia, pallor, mottled \nskin).\nPulse and respiratory rates\nIn childhood, a wide pulse pressure may be seen \nin patients with patent ductus arteriosus, aortic \nregurgitation and hyperthyroidism. The most common \ncauses of a narrow pulse pressure are subaortic and \naortic valve stenosis. Again, it is better to count the \npulse than to use an automated system, and the pulse \nis usually easily palpated in most children, so there are \nMale\nFemale\nSystolic \n(mmHg)\nDiastolic \n(mmHg)\nSystolic \n(mmHg)\nDiastolic \n(mmHg)\n3\n100\n59\n100\n61\n4\n102\n62\n101\n64\n5\n104\n65\n103\n66\n6\n105\n68\n104\n68\n7\n106\n70\n106\n69\n8\n107\n71\n108\n71\n9\n109\n72\n110\n72\n10\n111\n73\n112\n73\n11\n113\n74\n114\n74\n12\n115\n74\n116\n75\n13\n117\n75\n117\n76\n14\n120\n75\n119\n77\n15\n120\n76\n120\n78\n16\n120\n78\n120\n78\n17\n120\n80\n120\n78\n18\n120\n80\n120\n80\nBlood pressure values requiring further \nevaluation, according to age and sex\nTABLE 37.2 \n(Kaelber DC, Pickett F. Simple table to identify children and \nadolescents needing further evaluation of blood pressure. Pediatrics \n2009; 123:e972e4. With permission of the American Academy of \nPediatrics.)\n",
