    "text": "with loss of the light reflex (see Ch 32, Fig. 42.16), may signify acute otitis media (AOM) (also called otitis media with effusion—OME; see Ch 42). While genetic, infectious, immunological and environmental factors all play a role in this predisposition, the shape of the eustachian tube itself, which is shorter and less angled in children (see Fig. 37.22), means that it is easier for organisms to migrate from the nasopharynx to the middle ear and more difficult for the middle ear to drain.22 Although otoscopy should not hurt unless it is done incorrectly, it can be quite upsetting for younger children and is best performed while they are seated on their parent’s lap—and at the end of the examination. If necessary, have the parent lock the child’s lower limbs securely between their thighs (or even wrap a leg across the child’s legs) and use his or her arms to embrace the child warmly, holding them tight against their chest, \nWebbed neck in Turner’s syndrome \n(From Bouloux PM. Self-assessment picture tests. Medicine, vol 1. St \nLouis, MO: Mosby, 1996; Goljan EF. Rapid review laboratory testing in \nclinical medicine. St Louis, MO: Mosby, 2008.)\nFIGURE 37.21 \n",
