    "text": "678\t\nSECTION 10  PAEDIATRIC AND NEONATAL HISTORY AND EXAMINATION \n\t\nand those of us who regularly examine children need \nto be able to recognise potentially abused children (see \nTable 37.19 and List 37.5) and treat them at first \npresentation to prevent significant morbidity and \nmortality.\nPhysical abuse should always be a differential for \ninjuries in children. Suspicion may be raised during \nhistory taking, particularly if the caregiver has no \nexplanation for the child’s injuries, the explanation \nchanges over time or there is a delay in seeking care.\nInternal examination (using a speculum) is almost \nnever indicated in young girls and specialist advice \nshould be sought before attempting this, even in the \npostpubertal female.\nAnal and rectal examination\nAnal and rectal examination is fairly similar for all \nage groups. Check for an anteriorly placed anus, skin \ntags, haemorrhoids (uncommon in children) and \nanal fissures, which can be caused by constipation or \ninflammatory bowel disease. Although inspection can \nbe very important and informative, rectal examination \nis almost never indicated in children.\nTanner staging in puberty\nGenital examination in children and adolescents may \nbe performed as part of a pubertal assessment. In the \n1960s Tanner and colleagues developed a staging system \nthat is still used today (with minor modifications) to \nassess progression through puberty in both sexes (see \nTables 37.17 and 37.18). Generally, enlargement of the \ntestes is the first physical evidence of puberty in males \n(~98%) and the appearance of palpable breast tissue \nunder the areola is the first physical evidence of puberty \nin females (~80%; see Fig. 37.33). In the remainder of \nadolescents, pubic hair is the first physical evidence (see \nFig. 37.34).7 For a quick screening it is often sufficient \nto show the child Tanner staging illustrations and ask \nwhere they see their current stage best represented.\nPhysical examination for suspected child \nabuse (see Text box 37.3)\nUnfortunately, child abuse is one of the leading causes \nof injury-related mortality in infants and children,36 \n(Republished with permission of John Wiley & Sons Inc, from Tanner \nJM. Growth at adolescence, 2nd edn. Oxford, Blackwell Scientific/Wiley, \n1962, permission conveyed through Copyright Clearance Center, Inc.)\nSMR stage\nPubic hair\nBreasts\n1\nPreadolescent\nPreadolescent\n2\nSparse, lightly \npigmented, \nstraight, medial \nborder of labia\nBreast and papilla \nelevated as small \nmound; diameter \nof areola increased\n3\nDarker, beginning \nto curl, increased \namount\nBreast and areola \nenlarged, no \ncontour separation\n4\nCoarse, curly, \nabundant, but \nless than in adult\nAreola and papilla \nform secondary \nmound\n5\nAdult feminine \ntriangle, spread \nto medial surface \nof thighs\nMature, nipple \nprojects, areola \npart of general \nbreast contour\nSMR = sexual maturity rating.\nTABLE 37.17 \nClassification of sexual maturity in girls\nSMR stage\nPubic hair\nPenis\nTestes\n1\nNone\nPreadolescent\nPreadolescent\n2\nScanty, long, slightly pigmented\nMinimal \nchange / enlargement\nEnlarged scrotum, pink, \ntexture altered\n3\nDarker, starting to curl, small amount\nLengthens\nLarger\n4\nResembles adult type, but less quantity; coarse, curly\nLarger; glans and \nbreadth increase in size\nLarger, scrotum dark\n5\nAdult distribution, spread to medial surface of thighs\nAdult size\nAdult size\nSMR = sexual maturity rating.\nClassification of sexual maturity in boys\n(Republished with permission of John Wiley & Sons Inc, from Tanner JM. Growth at adolescence, 2nd edn. Oxford, Blackwell Scientific/Wiley, \n1962, permission conveyed through Copyright Clearance Center, Inc.)\nTABLE 37.18 \n",
