    "text": "754\t\nSECTION 11  Women’s health history and examination\n\t\nstress incontinence. Note the presence of vaginal atrophy \nin older women.\nProlapse\nUterovaginal prolapse is the protrusion of the uterus \nand / or the vaginal walls beyond their normal \nanatomical confines. Prolapse commonly occurs in \nAppearance of the vaginal wall\nAsk the patient to bear down; a cystocele (descent of \nthe bladder through the anterior vaginal wall) or \nrectocele (descent of the rectum through the posterior \nvaginal wall) or uterine prolapse may become apparent. \nThen ask the patient to cough; this may demonstrate \nPerineum\nFourchette\nVestibule\nLabium minus\nLabium majus\nFrenulum of clitoris\nClitoris\nMons pubis\nPubic hair\nPrepuce\nExternal urethral orifice \n(meatus)\nVaginal orifice\nHymen\nAnus\nPosterior commisure\nb\nSacral\npromontary\nFallopian tube\nSacrouterine\nligament\n Pouch of\nDouglas\nLevator ani muscle\nExternal\nanal sphincter\nAnus\nFornix of vagina\n \nCervix\nUreter\nOvarian ligament\nBody of uterus\n \nFundus of uterus\n \nBladder\nSymphisis pubis\nUrethra\nLabium minus\nLabium majus\nVagina\nClitoris\na\n(a) Lateral view, showing the relationship of the genitals to the rectum and bladder. \n(b) Position for examination \n(From Douglas G, Nicol F, Robertson C. Macleod’s clinical examination, 12th edn. Edinburgh: Churchill Livingstone, 2009.)\nFIGURE 40.3 \nFemale reproductive anatomy. \n",
