    "text": "CHAPTER 40  The gynaecological history and examination\t\n755\nPresentation\nCondition\nItchy, erythematous rash (endogenous)\nDermatitis\nAtopic/seborrhoeic\nAllergic or irritant induced (exogenous)\nChronic irritation, results in thickening and hypertrophy of skin, erythema, excoriations\nLichen simplex chronicus\nMucosa not involved\nItch, discomfort, white discharge, dyspareunia, dysuria\nCandidiasis\nHypersensitivity reaction on vulva\nRequires positive culture to confirm diagnosis\nWhitened parchment-like plaques\nLichen sclerosus (see Fig. 40.4)\nClassic hourglass appearance involving perianal skin\nLoss of labial/clitoral architecture, introital narrowing\nMay have tearing or subepithelial haemorrhage/petechiae\nMucosa not involved\nLichen simplex chronicus difficult to differentiate from lichen planus\nLichen planus\nIntroitus of vagina involved\nAdhesions and erosions may occur; not responsive to surgical division\nOral/gingival involvement possible\nItchy, scaly red plaques not as well demarcated as elsewhere on the skin; examine hair/\nscalp and nails also\nPsoriasis\nItching\nVulvar intraepithelial neoplasia\nDifferent patterns may be associated with red velvety area or area of lichenification\nItch, discharge, bleeding may be painful\nVulvar cancer (see Fig. 40.5)\nAssociated lymphadenopathy\nRaised ulcerated lesion\nTABLE 40.4 \nVulval skin conditions\nCondition\nCausative organism\nSymptoms\nSigns\nBacterial \nvaginosis\nAnaerobic organisms \nincluding Gardnerella \nspp\nSmelly vaginal discharge\nVulval irritation\nA typical thin homogeneous \nvaginal discharge\nChlamydial \nvulvovaginitis\nChlamydia trachomatis\nAsymptomatic or vaginal discharge\nIntermenstrual bleeding\nInflamed cervix\nContact bleeding\nTenderness on cervical \nmovement\nGenital herpes\nHerpes simplex virus \n(HSV) type 2\nVaginal discharge\nVulval pain\nDysuria\nUrinary retention\nSkin vesicles and multiple \nshallow skin ulcers\nInguinal lymphadenopathy\nGenital warts \n(condyloma \nacuminata)\nHuman papillomavirus\nPruritus\nVaginal discharge\nPapillomatous lesions over \nvulva, perineum and into \nvagina\nGonococcal \nvulvovaginitis\nNeisseria gonorrhoeae\nVaginal discharge\nMucopurulent vaginal \ndischarge\nTrichomoniasis\nTrichomonas vaginalis\nVaginal bleeding\nVaginal soreness\nPruritus\nGreen frothy, watery discharge\nVaginal \ncandidiasis\nCandida albicans\nIncreased or changed vaginal \ndischarge associated with soreness \nand itching in the vulva area\nWhite, curd-like collections \nattached to the vaginal \nepithelium\nTABLE 40.5 \nCommon organisms causing lower genital tract infections\n",
