    "text": "\t\nCHAPTER 42  The eyes, ears, nose and throat\t\n787\nFrom the lower end of the handle a bright cone of light \nshould be visible: the light reflex. The presence or \nabsence of the light reflex is not a sensitive or a specific \nsign of disease. Note the colour, transparency and any \nevidence of dilated blood vessels (hyperaemia—a sign \nof otitis media; see Fig. 42.16). Look for bulging or \nretraction of the tympanic membrane. Bulging can \nsuggest underlying fluid or pus in the middle ear. \nRetraction means a reduction in pressure in the middle \near and is a sign of a blocked Eustachian tube. \nPerforation of the tympanic membrane should be noted \n(see Fig. 42.17).\nIf a middle ear infection is suspected, pneumatic \nauriscopy can be useful. Use a speculum large enough \nto occlude the external canal snugly. Attach a rubber \nsqueeze bulb to the otoscope. When the bulb is squeezed \ngently, air pressure in the canal is increased and the \ntympanic membrane should move promptly inwards. \nAbsence of, or a decrease in, movement is a sign of \nfluid in the middle ear.\nTo test hearing, whisper numbers or words such \nas ‘mark’ or ‘park’ into one of the patient’s ears while \nthe other ear is distracted by movement of your finger \nin the auditory canal or the canal is occluded by pressure \non the tragus. It is important to stand behind the patient \nto prevent lip reading. Then repeat the process with \nthe other ear. With practice, the normal range of hearing \nis appreciated. If a patient says he or she is deaf, or the \nmembrane is a pearly grey colour. It is ovoid in shape \nand semitransparent (see Fig. 42.14). The upper fifth \nis called the pars flaccida and the lower four-fifths are \ncalled the pars tensa (see Fig. 42.15). The handle of \nthe malleus is often visible near the centre of the latter. \nLong process\nof incus\nPosterior\nsuperior\nquadrant\nPosterior\nAnterior\nAnterior\nsuperior\nquadrant\nLateral\nprocess \nof malleus\nAnterior posterior malleolar ligaments\nPars flaccida   \nCone of\nreflected light\nThe detail of the tympanic membrane \n(From Mir MA. Atlas of clinical diagnosis, 2nd edn. Edinburgh: \nSaunders, 2003.)\nFIGURE 42.15 \nOtitis media with hyperaemia of the \ntympanic membrane \n(From Mir MA. Atlas of clinical diagnosis, 2nd edn. Edinburgh: \nSaunders, 2003.)\nFIGURE 42.16 \nThe tympanic membrane as viewed through \nan otoscope \n(From Mir MA. Atlas of clinical diagnosis, 2nd edn. Edinburgh: \nSaunders, 2003.)\nFIGURE 42.14 \n",
