    }
  },
  {
    "text": "ECG Rhythm Interpretation\nModule II\nHow to Analyze a Rhythm\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 12,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 60,
      "word_count": 10,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Course Objectives\n• To recognize the normal rhythm of the \nheart - “Normal Sinus Rhythm.”\n• To recognize the 13 most common \nrhythm disturbances.\n• To recognize an acute myocardial \ninfarction on a 12-lead ECG.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 13,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 211,
      "word_count": 35,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Learning Modules\n• ECG Basics\n• How to Analyze a Rhythm\n• Normal Sinus Rhythm\n• Heart Arrhythmias\n• Diagnosing a Myocardial Infarction\n• Advanced 12-Lead Interpretation\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 14,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 169,
      "word_count": 27,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rhythm Analysis\n• Step 1: Calculate rate.\n• Step 2: Determine regularity.\n• Step 3: Assess the P waves.\n• Step 4: Determine PR interval.\n• Step 5: Determine QRS duration.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 15,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 171,
      "word_count": 31,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Step 1: Calculate Rate\n• Option 1\n– Count the # of R waves in a 6 second \nrhythm strip, then multiply by 10.\nInterpretation?\n9 x 10 = 90 bpm\n3 sec\n3 sec\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 16,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 153,
      "word_count": 35,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Step 1: Calculate Rate\n• Option 2 \n– Find a R wave that lands on a bold line.\nR wave\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 17,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 85,
      "word_count": 20,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Step 1: Calculate Rate\n• Option 2 (cont) \n– Memorize the sequence:\n300 - 150 - 100 - 75 - 60 - 50\nInterpretation?\n3\n0\n0\n1\n5\n0\n1\n0\n0\n7\n5\n6\n0\n5\n0\nApprox. 1 box less than \n100 = 95 bpm\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 18,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 182,
      "word_count": 48,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Step 2: Determine regularity\n• Look at the R-R distances (using a caliper or \nmarkings on a pen or paper).\n• Regular (are they equidistant apart)? \nOccasionally irregular? Regularly irregular? \nIrregularly irregular?\nInterpretation?\nRegular\nR\nR\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 19,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 245,
      "word_count": 36,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Step 3: Assess the P waves\n• Are there P waves?\n• Do the P waves all look alike?\n• Do the P waves occur at a regular rate?\n• Is there one P wave before each QRS?\nInterpretation? Normal P waves with 1 P \nwave for every QRS\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 20,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 222,
      "word_count": 49,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Step 4: Determine PR interval\n• Normal: 0.12 - 0.20 seconds.\n(3 - 5 boxes)\nInterpretation? 0.12 seconds\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 21,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 104,
      "word_count": 18,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Step 5: QRS duration\n• Normal: 0.04 - 0.12 seconds.\n(1 - 3 boxes)\nInterpretation? 0.08 seconds\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 22,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 95,
      "word_count": 17,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rhythm Summary\n• Rate\n90-95 bpm\n• Regularity\nregular\n• P waves\nnormal\n• PR interval\n0.12 s\n• QRS duration\n0.08 s\nInterpretation?\nNormal Sinus Rhythm\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 23,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 149,
      "word_count": 27,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "End of Module II\nHow to Analyze a Rhythm\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 24,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 41,
      "word_count": 9,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "ECG Rhythm Interpretation\nModule III\nNormal Sinus Rhythm\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 25,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 57,
      "word_count": 8,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Learning Modules\n• ECG Basics\n• How to Analyze a Rhythm\n• Normal Sinus Rhythm\n• Heart Arrhythmias\n• Diagnosing a Myocardial Infarction\n• Advanced 12-Lead Interpretation\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 26,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 169,
      "word_count": 27,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Normal Sinus Rhythm (NSR)\n• Etiology: the electrical impulse is formed \nin the SA node and conducted normally.\n• This is the normal rhythm of the heart; \nother rhythms that do not conduct via \nthe typical pathway are called \narrhythmias.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 27,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 238,
      "word_count": 40,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "NSR Parameters\n• Rate\n60 - 100 bpm\n• Regularity\nregular\n• P waves\nnormal\n• PR interval\n0.12 - 0.20 s\n• QRS duration\n0.04 - 0.12 s\nAny deviation from above is sinus \ntachycardia, sinus bradycardia or an \narrhythmia\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 28,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 214,
      "word_count": 41,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Arrhythmia Formation\nArrhythmias can arise from problems in \nthe:\n• Sinus node\n• Atrial cells\n• AV junction\n• Ventricular cells\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 29,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 128,
      "word_count": 21,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SA Node Problems\nThe SA Node can:\n• fire too slow\n• fire too fast\nSinus Bradycardia\nSinus Tachycardia\nSinus Tachycardia may be an appropriate\nresponse to stress.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 30,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 162,
      "word_count": 28,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Atrial Cell Problems\nAtrial cells can:\n• fire occasionally \nfrom a focus \n• fire continuously \ndue to a looping \nre-entrant circuit \nPremature Atrial \nContractions (PACs)\nAtrial Flutter \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 31,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 187,
      "word_count": 27,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Teaching Moment\n• A re-entrant \npathway occurs \nwhen an impulse \nloops and results \nin self-\nperpetuating \nimpulse \nformation.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 32,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 127,
      "word_count": 18,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Atrial Cell Problems\nAtrial cells can also:\n• fire continuously \nfrom multiple foci \nor\nfire continuously \ndue to multiple \nmicro re-entrant \n“wavelets”\nAtrial Fibrillation\nAtrial Fibrillation\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 33,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 193,
      "word_count": 26,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Teaching Moment\nMultiple micro re-\nentrant “wavelets” \nrefers to wandering \nsmall areas of \nactivation which \ngenerate fine chaotic \nimpulses. Colliding \nwavelets can, in turn, \ngenerate new foci of \nactivation.\nAtrial tissue\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 34,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 226,
      "word_count": 31,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "AV Junctional Problems\nThe AV junction can:\n• fire continuously \ndue to a looping \nre-entrant circuit \n• block impulses \ncoming from the \nSA Node\nParoxysmal     \nSupraventricular \nTachycardia\nAV Junctional Blocks\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 35,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 213,
      "word_count": 30,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Ventricular Cell Problems\nVentricular cells can:\n• fire occasionally \nfrom 1 or more foci\n• fire continuously \nfrom multiple foci\n• fire continuously \ndue to a looping \nre-entrant circuit \nPremature Ventricular \nContractions (PVCs)\nVentricular Fibrillation\nVentricular Tachycardia\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 36,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 281,
      "word_count": 37,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "End of Module III\nNormal Sinus Rhythm\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
