  },
  {
    "text": "ECG Rhythm Interpretation\nModule IV a\nSinus Rhythms and \nPremature Beats\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 38,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 73,
      "word_count": 11,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Learning Modules\n• ECG Basics\n• How to Analyze a Rhythm\n• Normal Sinus Rhythm\n• Heart Arrhythmias\n• Diagnosing a Myocardial Infarction\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 39,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 135,
      "word_count": 23,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Arrhythmias\n• Sinus Rhythms\n• Premature Beats\n• Supraventricular Arrhythmias\n• Ventricular Arrhythmias\n• AV Junctional Blocks\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 40,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 126,
      "word_count": 17,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Sinus Rhythms\n• Sinus Bradycardia\n• Sinus Tachycardia\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 41,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 54,
      "word_count": 8,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rhythm #1\n30 bpm\n• Rate?\n• Regularity?\nregular\nnormal\n0.10 s\n• P waves?\n• PR interval?\n0.12 s\n• QRS duration?\nInterpretation? Sinus Bradycardia\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 42,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 144,
      "word_count": 26,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Sinus Bradycardia\n• Deviation from NSR\n- Rate\n< 60 bpm\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 43,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 55,
      "word_count": 11,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Sinus Bradycardia\n• Etiology: SA node is depolarizing slower \nthan normal, impulse is conducted \nnormally (i.e. normal PR and QRS \ninterval).\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 44,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 142,
      "word_count": 21,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rhythm #2\n130 bpm\n• Rate?\n• Regularity?\nregular\nnormal\n0.08 s\n• P waves?\n• PR interval?\n0.16 s\n• QRS duration?\nInterpretation? Sinus Tachycardia\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 45,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 145,
      "word_count": 26,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Sinus Tachycardia\n• Deviation from NSR\n- Rate\n> 100 bpm\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 46,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 56,
      "word_count": 11,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Sinus Tachycardia\n• Etiology: SA node is depolarizing faster \nthan normal, impulse is conducted \nnormally.\n• Remember: sinus tachycardia is a \nresponse to physical or psychological \nstress, not a primary arrhythmia.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 47,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 216,
      "word_count": 31,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Premature Beats\n• Premature Atrial Contractions\n(PACs)\n• Premature Ventricular Contractions\n(PVCs)\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 48,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 99,
      "word_count": 12,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rhythm #3\n70 bpm\n• Rate?\n• Regularity?\noccasionally irreg.\n2/7 different contour\n0.08 s\n• P waves?\n• PR interval?\n0.14 s (except 2/7)\n• QRS duration?\nInterpretation? NSR with Premature Atrial \nContractions\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 49,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 206,
      "word_count": 34,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Premature Atrial Contractions\n• Deviation from NSR\n–These ectopic beats originate in the \natria (but not in the SA node), \ntherefore the contour of the P wave, \nthe PR interval, and the timing are \ndifferent than a normally generated \npulse from the SA node.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 50,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 259,
      "word_count": 44,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Premature Atrial Contractions\n• Etiology: Excitation of an atrial cell \nforms an impulse that is then conducted \nnormally through the AV node and \nventricles.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 51,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 159,
      "word_count": 24,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Teaching Moment\n• When an impulse originates anywhere in \nthe atria (SA node, atrial cells, AV node, \nBundle of His) and then is conducted \nnormally through the ventricles, the QRS \nwill be narrow (0.04 - 0.12 s).\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 52,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 214,
      "word_count": 37,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rhythm #4\n60 bpm\n• Rate?\n• Regularity?\noccasionally irreg.\nnone for 7th QRS\n0.08 s (7th wide)\n• P waves?\n• PR interval?\n0.14 s\n• QRS duration?\nInterpretation? Sinus Rhythm with 1 PVC\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 53,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 183,
      "word_count": 35,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "PVCs\n• Deviation from NSR\n– Ectopic beats originate in the ventricles \nresulting in wide and bizarre QRS \ncomplexes.\n– When there are more than 1 premature \nbeats and look alike, they are called \n“uniform”. When they look different, they are \ncalled “multiform”.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 54,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 263,
      "word_count": 43,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "PVCs\n• Etiology: One or more ventricular cells \nare depolarizing and the impulses are \nabnormally conducting through the \nventricles.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 55,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 134,
      "word_count": 19,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Teaching Moment\n• When an impulse originates in a \nventricle, conduction through the \nventricles will be inefficient and the QRS \nwill be wide and bizarre.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 56,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 156,
      "word_count": 25,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Ventricular Conduction\nNormal\nSignal moves rapidly \nthrough the ventricles\nAbnormal\nSignal moves slowly \nthrough the ventricles\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 57,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 128,
      "word_count": 16,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "PREMATURE VENTRICULAR CONTRACTION: VENTRICULAR BIGEMINY\nPREMATURE VENTRICULAR CONTRACTION: UNIFORM\n\nAA fa ABBE GE IO ee\n\nPREMATURE VENTRICULAR CONTRACTION: MULTIFORM\n\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 58,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 167,
      "word_count": 19,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "PREMATURE VENTRICULAR CONTRACTION: VENTRICULAR BIGEMINY\n\n* In ventricular bigeminy, the PVC occurs with every other beat.\n\nee a :\n\nPREMATURE VENTRICULAR CONTRACTION: VENTRICULAR TRIGEMINY\n\n* In ventricular trigeminy, the PVC occurs with every third beat.\n\nothe Sarees\n\nee\nrol\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 59,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 276,
      "word_count": 39,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "PREMATURE VENTRICULAR CONTRACTION: VENTRICULAR QUADRIGEMINY\n\n« In ventricular quadrigeminy, the PVC occurs with every fourth beat.\n\not\n\nPREMATURE VENTRICULAR CONTRACTION: COUPLETS\n\n¢ Ina rhythm with PVC couplets, the PVCs occur in pairs.\n\nLf\n\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 60,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 243,
      "word_count": 33,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "End of Module IV a\nSinus Rhythms and \nPremature Beats\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 61,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 54,
      "word_count": 10,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "ECG Rhythm Interpretation\nModule IV b\nSupraventricular and \nVentricular Arrhythmias\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 62,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 84,
      "word_count": 10,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Learning Modules\n• ECG Basics\n• How to Analyze a Rhythm\n• Normal Sinus Rhythm\n• Heart Arrhythmias\n• Diagnosing a Myocardial Infarction\n• Advanced 12-Lead Interpretation\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 63,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 169,
      "word_count": 27,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Arrhythmias\n• Sinus Rhythms\n• Premature Beats\n• Supraventricular Arrhythmias\n• Ventricular Arrhythmias\n• AV Junctional Blocks\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 64,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 126,
      "word_count": 17,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Supraventricular Arrhythmias\n• Atrial Fibrillation\n• Atrial Flutter\n• Paroxysmal Supraventricular \nTachycardia\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 65,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 111,
      "word_count": 12,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rhythm #5\n100 bpm\n• Rate?\n• Regularity?\nirregularly irregular\nnone\n0.06 s\n• P waves?\n• PR interval?\nnone\n• QRS duration?\nInterpretation? Atrial Fibrillation\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 66,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 157,
      "word_count": 26,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Atrial Fibrillation\n• Deviation from NSR\n–No organized atrial depolarization, so \nno normal P waves (impulses are not \noriginating from the sinus node).\n–Atrial activity is chaotic (resulting in an \nirregularly irregular rate).\n–Common, affects 2-4%, up to 5-10% if \n> 80 years old\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 67,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 282,
      "word_count": 44,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Atrial Fibrillation\n• Etiology: Recent theories suggest that it \nis due to multiple re-entrant wavelets \nconducted between the R & L atria. \nEither way, impulses are formed in a \ntotally unpredictable fashion. The AV \nnode allows some of the impulses to \npass through at variable intervals (so \nrhythm is irregularly irregular).\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 68,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 329,
      "word_count": 51,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "ATRIAL FIBRILLATION\n* Rapid, erratic electrical discharge comes from multiple atrial ectopic foci.\n* No organized atrial depolarization are detectable.\n\nirregular RR intervals\na FI P| sir | s a * eter | | : *\n\nae 4 in 1. a\n\nRate: Atrial: =350 bpm; ventricular: varlable Rhythm: Irregular\n\nP Waves: No true P waves; chaotic PR Interval: None QRS: Normal (0.06—0.10 sec)\natrial activity\n\nAtrial fibrillation is often a chronic arrhythmia associated with underlying heart disease.\n\nSigns and symptoms depend on the ventricular response rate.\n\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 69,
      "type": "table",
      "char_count": 540,
      "word_count": 86,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rhythm #6\n70 bpm\n• Rate?\n• Regularity?\nregular\nflutter waves\n0.06 s\n• P waves?\n• PR interval?\nnone\n• QRS duration?\nInterpretation? Atrial Flutter\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 70,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 146,
      "word_count": 26,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Atrial Flutter\n• Deviation from NSR\n–No P waves. Instead flutter waves (note \n“sawtooth” pattern) are formed at a rate \nof 250 - 350 bpm.\n–Only some impulses conduct through \nthe AV node (usually every other \nimpulse).\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 71,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 219,
      "word_count": 37,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Atrial Flutter\n• Etiology: Reentrant pathway in the right \natrium with every 2nd, 3rd or 4th \nimpulse generating a QRS (others are \nblocked in the AV node as the node \nrepolarizes).\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 72,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 182,
      "word_count": 31,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "* The AV node conducts impulses to the ventricles at a 2:1, 3:1, 4:1, or greater ratio (rarely 1:1).\n* The degree of AV block may be consistent or variable.\n\nFie payee.\n\ndicdealecAedo id ec\n\nRate: Atrial: 250-350 bpm; ventricular: varlable. Rhythm: Atrial: regular; ventricular: variable\n\nP Waves: Flutter waves have a PR Interval: Variable QRS: Usually normal (0.06—\n\nsaw-toothed appearance; some 0.10 sec), but may appear\nmay not be visible, being widened if flutter waves are\n\nburled in the ORS burled in ORS\n\n©\n\nThe presence of Aiutter may be the first indication of cardiac disease.\n\nSigns and symptoms depend on the ventricular response rate.\n\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 73,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 650,
      "word_count": 106,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rhythm #7\n74 148 bpm\n• Rate?\n• Regularity?\nRegular regular\nNormal none\n0.08 s\n• P waves?\n• PR interval?\n0.16 s none\n• QRS duration?\nInterpretation? Paroxysmal Supraventricular \nTachycardia (PSVT)\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 74,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 200,
      "word_count": 32,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "PSVT\n• Deviation from NSR\n–The heart rate suddenly speeds up, \noften triggered by a PAC (not seen \nhere) and the P waves are lost.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 75,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 131,
      "word_count": 25,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "PSVT\n• Etiology: There are several types of \nPSVT but all originate above the \nventricles (therefore the QRS is narrow). \n• Most common: abnormal conduction in \nthe AV node (reentrant circuit looping in \nthe AV node).\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 76,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 218,
      "word_count": 36,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "PAROXYSMAL SUPRAVENTRICULAR TACHYCARDIA (PSVT)\n\n« PSVT is a rapid rhythm that starts and stops suddenly.\n\n* For accurate interpretation, the beginning or end of the PSVT must be seen.\n* PSVT is sometimes called paroxysmal atrial tachycardia (PAT).\n\nSudden auat of svt”\n\naA : in ALAN Wy A\n\nRate: 150-250 bpm Rhythm: Irregular\n\nP Waves: Frequently buried In PR Interval: Usually not possible QRS: Normal (0.06—-0.10 sec)\n\npreceding T waves and difficult to measure but may be wide If abnormally\nlo see conducted through ventricles\n\n©\n\nThe patient may feel palpitations, dizziness, lightheadedness, or anxiety.\n\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 77,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 609,
      "word_count": 95,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Ventricular Arrhythmias\n• Ventricular Tachycardia\n• Ventricular Fibrillation\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 78,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 77,
      "word_count": 8,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rhythm #8\n160 bpm\n• Rate?\n• Regularity?\nregular\nnone\nwide (> 0.12 sec)\n• P waves?\n• PR interval?\nnone\n• QRS duration?\nInterpretation? Ventricular Tachycardia\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 79,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 158,
      "word_count": 27,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Ventricular Tachycardia\n• Deviation from NSR\n–Impulse is originating in the ventricles \n(no P waves, wide QRS).\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 80,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 112,
      "word_count": 17,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Ventricular Tachycardia\n• Etiology: There is a re-entrant pathway \nlooping in a ventricle (most common \ncause). \n• Ventricular tachycardia can sometimes \ngenerate enough cardiac output to \nproduce a pulse; at other times no pulse \ncan be felt.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 81,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 244,
      "word_count": 37,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rhythm #9\nnone\n• Rate?\n• Regularity?\nirregularly irreg.\nnone\nwide, if recognizable \n• P waves?\n• PR interval?\nnone\n• QRS duration?\nInterpretation? Ventricular Fibrillation\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 82,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 172,
      "word_count": 26,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "VENTRICULAR TACHYCARDIA (VT): MONOMORPHIC\n\n« In monomorphic VT, QRS complexes have the same shape and amplitude.\n\nTOME ET\nHa aan Aggie gee pang al\n\n| a He P) |\nES ta ERED ES Hy ip |\n\nie Bie \\ a pee ae 4\n\n. - s (\\ is EX\nset arn tate y | ie\n\nRate: 100-250 bpm Rhythm: Regular\n\nP Waves: None or not assoclated PR Interval: None QRS: Wide (0.10 sec),\nwith the QRS bizarre appearance\n\nit is important to confirm the presence or absence of pulses because monomorphic VT may be perfusing or\nnonperfusing\n\n¢\n\nMonomorphic VT will probably deteriorate into VF or unstable VT if Sustained and not treated.\n\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 83,
      "type": "table",
      "char_count": 596,
      "word_count": 113,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "VENTRICULAR TACHYCARDIA (VT): POLYMORPHIC\n* In polymorphic VT, QRS complexes vary in shape and amplitude.\n¢ The QT interval is normal or long.\n\nRate: 100-250 bpm Rhythm: Regular or Irregular\n\nP Waves: None or not associated PR Interval: None QRS: Wide (=0.10 sec),\nwith the ORS bizarre appearance\n\n.\n\nIt is important to confirm the presence or absence of pulses because polymorphic VT may be perfusing or\nnonperfusing.\n\nConsider electrolyte abnormalities as a possible c\n\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 84,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 472,
      "word_count": 75,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Ventricular Fibrillation\n• Deviation from NSR\n–Completely abnormal.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 85,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 68,
      "word_count": 8,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Ventricular Fibrillation\n• Etiology: The ventricular cells are \nexcitable and depolarizing randomly.\n• Rapid drop in cardiac output and death \noccurs if not quickly reversed \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 86,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 175,
      "word_count": 25,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "End of Module IV b\nSupraventricular and \nVentricular Arrhythmias\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 87,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 65,
      "word_count": 9,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "ECG Rhythm Interpretation\nModule IV c\nAV Junctional Blocks\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 88,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 59,
      "word_count": 9,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Course Objectives\n• To recognize the normal rhythm of the \nheart - “Normal Sinus Rhythm.”\n• To recognize the 13 most common \nrhythm disturbances.\n• To recognize an acute myocardial \ninfarction on a 12-lead ECG.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 89,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 211,
      "word_count": 35,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Learning Modules\n• ECG Basics\n• How to Analyze a Rhythm\n• Normal Sinus Rhythm\n• Heart Arrhythmias\n• Diagnosing a Myocardial Infarction\n• Advanced 12-Lead Interpretation\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 90,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 169,
      "word_count": 27,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Arrhythmias\n• Sinus Rhythms\n• Premature Beats\n• Supraventricular Arrhythmias\n• Ventricular Arrhythmias\n• AV Junctional Blocks\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 91,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 126,
      "word_count": 17,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "AV Nodal Blocks\n• 1st Degree AV Block\n• 2nd Degree AV Block, Type I\n• 2nd Degree AV Block, Type II\n• 3rd Degree AV Block\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 92,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 121,
      "word_count": 27,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rhythm #10\n60 bpm\n• Rate?\n• Regularity?\nregular\nnormal\n0.08 s\n• P waves?\n• PR interval?\n0.36 s\n• QRS duration?\nInterpretation? 1st Degree AV Block\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 93,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 147,
      "word_count": 28,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "1st Degree AV Block\n• Deviation from NSR\n–PR Interval\n> 0.20 s\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 94,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 63,
      "word_count": 13,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "1st Degree AV Block\n• Etiology: Prolonged conduction delay in \nthe AV node or Bundle of His.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 95,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 93,
      "word_count": 17,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "FIRST-DEGREE AV BLOCK\n\nRate: Depends on rate of underlying rhythm Rhythm: Regular\n\nP Waves: Normal (upright and PR Interval: Prolonged (>0.20 sec) QRS: Normal (0.06-0.10 sec)\nuniform)\n\nUsually a first-degree AV block is benign, but if associated with an acute Ml it may lead to further AV defects.\n\nOften AV block is caused by medications that prolong AV conduction; these include digoxin, calcium channel\nblockers, and beta blockers.\n\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 96,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 436,
      "word_count": 68,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rhythm #11\n50 bpm\n• Rate?\n• Regularity?\nregularly irregular\nnl, but 4th no QRS\n0.08 s\n• P waves?\n• PR interval?\nlengthens\n• QRS duration?\nInterpretation? 2nd Degree AV Block, Type I\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 97,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 182,
      "word_count": 34,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SECOND-DEGREE AV BLOCK: TYPE | (MOBITZ | OR WENCKEBACH)\n\n« PR intervals become progressively longer until one P wave is totally blocked and produces no QRS complex.\nAfter a pause, during which the AV node recovers, this cycle is repeated.\n\n| Blocked: beat\n\nSaarland ae. Bice\n\nRate: Depends on rate of underlying rhythm Rhythm: Atrial: regular; ventricular: irregular\n\nP Waves: Normal (upright and PR Interval: Progressively longer QRS: Normal (0.06—0.10 sec)\nuniform), more P waves than QRS until one P wave Is blocked and a\nORS Is dropped\n\nThis rhythm may be caused by medication such as beta blockers, digoxin, and calcium channel blockers.\nIschemia involving the right coronary artery is another cause.\n\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 98,
      "type": "table",
      "char_count": 707,
      "word_count": 113,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "2nd Degree AV Block, Type I\n• Deviation from NSR\n–PR interval progressively lengthens, \nthen the impulse is completely blocked \n(P wave not followed by QRS).\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 99,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 158,
      "word_count": 26,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "2nd Degree AV Block, Type I\n• Etiology: Each successive atrial impulse \nencounters a longer and longer delay in \nthe AV node until one impulse (usually \nthe 3rd or 4th) fails to make it through \nthe AV node.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 100,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 208,
      "word_count": 38,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SECOND-DEGREE AV BLOCK: TYPE | (MOBITZ | OR WENCKEBACH)\n\n« PR intervals become progressively longer until one P wave is totally blocked and produces no QRS complex.\nAfter a pause, during which the AV node recovers, this cycle is repeated.\n\nBlocked beat\n| x\n\nRate: Depends on rate of underlying rhythm Rhythm: Atrial: regular; ventricular: irregular\n\nP Waves: Normal (upright and PR Interval: Progressively longer QRS: Normal (0.06—0.10 sec)\nuniform), more P waves than QRS _ until one P wave Is blocked and a\nORS Is dropped\n\n€\n\nThis rhythm may be caused by medication such as beta blockers, digoxin, and calcium channel blockers.\nIschemia involving the right coronary artery is another cause.\n\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 101,
      "type": "table",
      "char_count": 694,
      "word_count": 113,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rhythm #12\n40 bpm\n• Rate?\n• Regularity?\nregular\nnl, 2 of 3 no QRS\n0.08 s\n• P waves?\n• PR interval?\n0.14 s\n• QRS duration?\nInterpretation? 2nd Degree AV Block, Type II\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 102,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 167,
      "word_count": 35,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "2nd Degree AV Block, Type II\n• Deviation from NSR\n–Occasional P waves are completely \nblocked (P wave not followed by QRS).\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 103,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 124,
      "word_count": 22,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "2nd Degree AV Block, Type II\n• Etiology: Conduction is all or nothing \n(no prolongation of PR interval); \ntypically block occurs in the Bundle of \nHis.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 104,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 152,
      "word_count": 26,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SECOND-DEGREE AV BLOCK: TYPE Il (MOBITZ Il)\n\n* Conduction ratio (P waves to QRS complexes) is commonly 2:1, 3:1, 4:1, or variable.\n* ORS complexes are usually wide because this block usually involves both bundle branches.\n\n| \\\n\n_\nPF\nU\n\nRate: Atrial: usually 60-100 bpm; ventricular: Rhythm: Atrial regular and ventricular may be\nslower than atrial rate regular or irregular\n\nP Waves: Normal (upright and PR Interval: Normal or prolonged QRS: May be normal, but\n\nuniform); more P waves than but constant usually wide (>0.10 sec) if the\nORSs bundle branches are involved\n\n\\\nResulting bradycardia can compromise cardiac output and lead to complete AV block. This rhythm often occurs\nwith cardiac ischemia or an Ml\n\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 105,
      "type": "table",
      "char_count": 712,
      "word_count": 117,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rhythm #13\n40 bpm\n• Rate?\n• Regularity?\nregular\nno relation to QRS\nwide (> 0.12 s)\n• P waves?\n• PR interval?\nnone\n• QRS duration?\nInterpretation? 3rd Degree AV Block\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 106,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 166,
      "word_count": 32,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "3rd Degree AV Block\n• Deviation from NSR\n–The P waves are completely blocked in \nthe AV junction; QRS complexes \noriginate independently from below the \njunction.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 107,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 163,
      "word_count": 26,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "3rd Degree AV Block\n• Etiology: There is complete block of \nconduction in the AV junction, so the \natria and ventricles form impulses \nindependently of each other. Without \nimpulses from the atria, the ventricles \nown intrinsic pacemaker kicks in at \naround 30 - 45 beats/minute.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 108,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 280,
      "word_count": 45,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "THIRD-DEGREE AV BLOCK\n\n*« Conduction between the atria and the ventricles is totally absent because of complete electrical block at or\nbelow the AV node. This is known as AV dissociation.\n*« “Complete heart block” is another name for this rhythm.\n\nRate: Atrial: 60-100 bpm; ventricular: 40-60 bpm Rhythm: Usually regular, but atria and ventricles act\nif escape focus Is junctional, <40 bpm If escape Independently\nfocus Is ventricular\n\nP Waves: Normal (upright and PR Interval: Varles greatly QRS: Normal If ventricles are\n\nuniform); may be superimposed activated by junctional escape\n\non ORS complexes or T waves focus; wide if escape focus Is\nventricular\n\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 109,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 658,
      "word_count": 104,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Remember\n• When an impulse originates in a ventricle, \nconduction through the ventricles will be \ninefficient and the QRS will be wide and \nbizarre.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 110,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 149,
      "word_count": 24,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "End of Module IV c\nAV Junctional Blocks\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 111,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 40,
      "word_count": 8,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "ECG Rhythm Interpretation\nModule V\nAcute Myocardial Infarction\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 112,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 63,
      "word_count": 8,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Course Objectives\n• To recognize the normal rhythm of the \nheart - “Normal Sinus Rhythm.”\n• To recognize the 13 most common \nheart arrhythmias.\n• To recognize an acute myocardial \ninfarction on a 12-lead ECG.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 113,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 209,
      "word_count": 35,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Learning Modules\n• ECG Basics\n• How to Analyze a Rhythm\n• Normal Sinus Rhythm\n• Heart Arrhythmias\n• Diagnosing a Myocardial Infarction\n• Advanced 12-Lead Interpretation\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 114,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 169,
      "word_count": 27,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Diagnosing a MI\nTo diagnose a myocardial infarction you \nneed to go beyond looking at a rhythm \nstrip and obtain a 12-Lead ECG.\nRhythm \nStrip\n12-Lead \nECG\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 115,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 155,
      "word_count": 27,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "The 12-Lead ECG\n• The 12-Lead ECG sees the heart \nfrom 12 different views.\n• Therefore, the 12-Lead ECG helps \nyou see what is happening in \ndifferent portions of the heart.\n• The rhythm strip is only 1 of these 12 \nviews.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 116,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 223,
      "word_count": 42,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "The 12-Leads\nThe 12-leads include:\n–3 Limb leads\n(I, II, III)\n–3 Augmented leads\n(aVR, aVL, aVF)\n–6 Precordial leads\n(V1- V6)\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 117,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 126,
      "word_count": 22,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Views of the Heart\nSome leads get a \ngood view of the:\nAnterior portion \nof the heart\nLateral portion \nof the heart\nInferior portion \nof the heart\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 118,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 147,
      "word_count": 27,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "ST Elevation\nOne way to \ndiagnose an \nacute MI is to \nlook for \nelevation of \nthe ST \nsegment.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 119,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 95,
      "word_count": 18,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "ST Elevation (cont)\nElevation of the \nST segment \n(greater than 1 \nsmall box) in 2 \nleads is \nconsistent with a \nmyocardial \ninfarction.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 120,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 137,
      "word_count": 22,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Anterior View of the Heart\nThe anterior portion of the heart is best \nviewed using leads V1- V4.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 121,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 97,
      "word_count": 18,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Anterior Myocardial Infarction\nIf you see changes in leads V1 - V4\nthat are consistent with a myocardial \ninfarction, you can conclude that it is \nan anterior wall myocardial infarction.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 122,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 187,
      "word_count": 30,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Putting it all Together\nDo you think this person is having a \nmyocardial infarction. If so, where?\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 123,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 99,
      "word_count": 17,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Interpretation\nYes, this person is having an acute anterior \nwall myocardial infarction.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 124,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 89,
      "word_count": 12,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "* Occlusion of the left coronary artery—left anterior\ndescending branch\n\n*« ECG changes: ST segment elevation with tall T\nwaves and taller-than-normal R waves in leads V,\nand V,; reciprocal changes in II, III, and aVF\n\nVe\n\npgesend sors Steet\n\nAnterior myocardial infarction.\n\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 125,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 276,
      "word_count": 43,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Other MI Locations\nNow that you know where to look for an \nanterior wall myocardial infarction let’s \nlook at how you would determine if the MI \ninvolves the lateral wall or the inferior wall \nof the heart.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 126,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 207,
      "word_count": 37,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Other MI Locations\nFirst, take a look \nagain at this \npicture of the heart.\nAnterior portion \nof the heart\nLateral portion \nof the heart\nInferior portion \nof the heart\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 127,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 168,
      "word_count": 29,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Other MI Locations\nSecond, remember that the 12-leads of the ECG look at \ndifferent portions of the heart. The limb and augmented \nleads “see” electrical activity moving inferiorly (II, III and \naVF), to the left (I, aVL) and to the right (aVR). Whereas, the \nprecordial leads “see” electrical activity in the  posterior to \nanterior direction.\nLimb Leads\nAugmented Leads\nPrecordial Leads\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 128,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 389,
      "word_count": 61,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Other MI Locations\nNow, using these 3 diagrams let’s figure where \nto look for a lateral wall and inferior wall MI.\nLimb Leads\nAugmented Leads\nPrecordial Leads\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 129,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 160,
      "word_count": 27,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Anterior MI\nRemember the anterior portion of the heart is \nbest viewed using leads V1- V4.\nLimb Leads\nAugmented Leads\nPrecordial Leads\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 130,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 135,
      "word_count": 22,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Lateral MI\nSo what leads do you think \nthe lateral portion of the \nheart is best viewed? \nLimb Leads\nAugmented Leads\nPrecordial Leads\nLeads I, aVL, and V5- V6\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 131,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 159,
      "word_count": 29,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Inferior MI\nNow how about the \ninferior portion of the \nheart? \nLimb Leads\nAugmented Leads\nPrecordial Leads\nLeads II, III and aVF\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 132,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 130,
      "word_count": 22,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Putting it all Together\nNow, where do you think this person is \nhaving a myocardial infarction?\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 133,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 96,
      "word_count": 16,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Inferior Wall MI\nThis is an inferior MI. Note the ST elevation \nin leads II, III and aVF.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 134,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 90,
      "word_count": 18,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": " SEPTAL MYOCARDIAL INFARCTION\n\nOcclusion of the left coronary artery—left anterior\ndescending branch\n\nECG changes: pathological Q waves; absence of nor-\nmal R waves in leads V, and V,\n\nFigure 10.14 @ Septal myocardial infarction.\n\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 135,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 231,
      "word_count": 34,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Putting it all Together\n\nHow about now?\n\nO7-MAR-1 64 (24 big\nMale = Chaesdian\n\n‘ av i aa | : I\n\nPA ee eee Moa aera ey este Eb a VEE\n\ni ¥a || ¥6\n\n1\nID: OTRO eS 2s LeMay -1S05 WSS\n\n© 1997 Frank G. Yanowitz, M.D.\n\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 136,
      "type": "table",
      "char_count": 211,
      "word_count": 49,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "LATERAL MYOCARDIAL INFARCTION\n\n* Occlusion of the left coronary artery—circumflex\nbranch\n\n« ECG changes: ST segment elevation in leads I, aVL,\nV,, and V,; reciprocal ST segment depression in V,,\nV,, and V,\n\n@ Lateral myocardial infarction.\n\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 137,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 241,
      "word_count": 37,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Anterolateral MI\nThis person’s MI involves both the anterior wall \n(V2-V4) and the lateral wall (V5-V6, I, and aVL)!\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 138,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 117,
      "word_count": 19,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "@ Posterior myocardial infarction.\n\nPOSTERIOR MYOCARDIAL INFARCTION\n\n*\n\nOcclusion of the right coronary artery (posterior\ndescending branch) or the left circumflex artery\nUsually produces tall R waves and ST segment\ndepression in leads V,, V,, V;, and V,, Complica-\ntions may include left ventricular dysfunction.\nYou may need to view the true posterior leads, V,\nand V, (used in the 15-lead ECG) for definite diag\nnosis of an acute posterior MI. In these leads you\nwill see ST segment elevation.\n\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 139,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 498,
      "word_count": 80,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "ECG Rhythm Interpretation\nModule VI\nAdvanced 12-Lead Interpretation\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 140,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 68,
      "word_count": 8,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Course Objectives\n• To recognize the normal rhythm of the \nheart - “Normal Sinus Rhythm.”\n• To recognize the 13 most common \nheart arrhythmias.\n• To recognize an acute myocardial \ninfarction on a 12-lead ECG.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 141,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 209,
      "word_count": 35,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Learning Modules\n• ECG Basics\n• How to Analyze a Rhythm\n• Normal Sinus Rhythm\n• Heart Arrhythmias\n• Diagnosing a Myocardial Infarction\n• Advanced 12-Lead Interpretation\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 142,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 169,
      "word_count": 27,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "The 12-Lead ECG\nThe 12-Lead ECG contains a wealth of \ninformation. In Module V you learned that \nST segment elevation in two leads is \nsuggestive of an acute myocardial \ninfarction. In this module we will cover:\n–ST Elevation and non-ST Elevation MIs\n–Left Ventricular Hypertrophy\n–Bundle Branch Blocks\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 143,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 303,
      "word_count": 48,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "ST Elevation and \nnon-ST Elevation MIs\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 144,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 39,
      "word_count": 6,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "ST Elevation and non-ST Elevation MIs\n• When myocardial blood supply is abruptly \nreduced or cut off to a region of the heart, a \nsequence of injurious events occur beginning \nwith ischemia (inadequate tissue perfusion), \nfollowed by necrosis (infarction), and eventual \nfibrosis (scarring) if the blood supply isn't \nrestored in an appropriate period of time.\n• The ECG changes over time with each of \nthese events…\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 145,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 417,
      "word_count": 66,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "ECG Changes\nWays the ECG can change include:\nAppearance \nof pathologic \nQ-waves\nT-waves\npeaked      flattened     inverted\nST elevation & \ndepression\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 146,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 150,
      "word_count": 20,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "ECG Changes & the Evolving MI\nThere are two \ndistinct patterns \nof ECG change \ndepending if the \ninfarction is:\n–ST Elevation (Transmural or Q-wave), or\n–Non-ST Elevation (Subendocardial or non-Q-wave)\nNon-ST Elevation\nST Elevation\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 147,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 232,
      "word_count": 34,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "ST Elevation Infarction\nST depression, peaked T-waves, \nthen T-wave inversion\nThe ECG changes seen with a ST elevation infarction are:\nBefore injury Normal ECG\nST elevation & appearance of \nQ-waves \nST segments and T-waves return to \nnormal, but Q-waves persist\nIschemia\nInfarction\nFibrosis\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 148,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 291,
      "word_count": 43,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "ST Elevation Infarction\nHere’s a diagram depicting an evolving infarction:\nA.  Normal ECG prior to MI \nB.  Ischemia from coronary artery occlusion \nresults in ST depression (not shown) and \npeaked T-waves\nC.  Infarction from ongoing ischemia results in \nmarked ST elevation\nD/E.  Ongoing infarction with appearance of \npathologic Q-waves and T-wave inversion\nF.  Fibrosis (months later) with persistent Q-\nwaves, but normal ST segment and T-\nwaves \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 149,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 449,
      "word_count": 67,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "ST Elevation Infarction\nHere’s an ECG of an inferior MI:\nLook at the \ninferior leads \n(II, III, aVF).\nQuestion:\nWhat ECG \nchanges do \nyou see?\nST elevation \nand Q-waves\nExtra credit:\nWhat is the \nrhythm?\nAtrial fibrillation (irregularly irregular with narrow QRS)!\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 150,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 265,
      "word_count": 42,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Non-ST Elevation Infarction\nHere’s an ECG of an inferior MI later in time:\nNow what do \nyou see in the \ninferior leads?\nST elevation,   \nQ-waves and   \nT-wave \ninversion\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 151,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 170,
      "word_count": 28,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Non-ST Elevation Infarction\nST depression & T-wave inversion\nThe ECG changes seen with a non-ST elevation infarction are:\nBefore injury Normal ECG\nST depression & T-wave inversion \nST returns to baseline, but T-wave \ninversion persists\nIschemia\nInfarction\nFibrosis\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 152,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 265,
      "word_count": 38,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Non-ST Elevation Infarction\nHere’s an ECG of an evolving non-ST elevation MI:\nNote the ST \ndepression \nand T-wave \ninversion in \nleads V2-V6.\nQuestion:\nWhat area of \nthe heart is \ninfarcting?\nAnterolateral\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 153,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 206,
      "word_count": 31,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Left Ventricular Hypertrophy\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 154,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 29,
      "word_count": 3,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Left Ventricular Hypertrophy\nCompare these two 12-lead ECGs. What stands \nout as different with the second one?\nNormal\nLeft Ventricular Hypertrophy\nAnswer: The QRS complexes are very tall \n(increased voltage)\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 155,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 209,
      "word_count": 30,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Left Ventricular Hypertrophy\nWhy is left ventricular hypertrophy characterized by tall \nQRS complexes?\nLVH\nECHOcardiogram\nIncreased QRS voltage\nAs the heart muscle wall thickens there is an increase in \nelectrical forces moving through the myocardium resulting \nin increased QRS voltage.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 156,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 288,
      "word_count": 40,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Left Ventricular Hypertrophy\n• Criteria exists to diagnose LVH using a 12-lead ECG.\n– For example:\n• The R wave in V5 or V6 plus the S wave in V1 or V2 \nexceeds 35 mm.\n• However, for now, all \nyou need to know is \nthat the QRS voltage \nincreases with LVH.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 157,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 256,
      "word_count": 52,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Bundle Branch Blocks\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 158,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 21,
      "word_count": 3,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Bundle Branch Blocks\nTurning our attention to bundle branch blocks… \nRemember normal \nimpulse conduction is\nSA node \nAV node \nBundle of His \nBundle Branches \nPurkinje fibers\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 159,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 178,
      "word_count": 30,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Normal Impulse Conduction\nSinoatrial node\nAV node\nBundle of His\nBundle Branches\nPurkinje fibers\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 160,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 96,
      "word_count": 14,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Bundle Branch Blocks\nSo, depolarization of \nthe Bundle Branches \nand Purkinje fibers are \nseen as the QRS \ncomplex on the ECG.\nTherefore, a conduction \nblock of the Bundle \nBranches would be \nreflected as a change in \nthe QRS complex.\nRight \nBBB\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 161,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 246,
      "word_count": 41,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Bundle Branch Blocks\nWith Bundle Branch Blocks you will see two changes \non the ECG.\n1. QRS complex widens (> 0.12 sec). \n2. QRS morphology changes (varies depending on ECG lead, \nand if it is a right vs. left bundle branch block).\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 162,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 232,
      "word_count": 42,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Bundle Branch Blocks\nWhy does the QRS complex widen?\nWhen the conduction \npathway is blocked it \nwill take longer for \nthe electrical signal \nto pass throughout \nthe ventricles.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 163,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 178,
      "word_count": 28,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Right Bundle Branch Blocks\nWhat QRS morphology is characteristic?\nV1\nFor RBBB the wide QRS complex assumes a \nunique, virtually diagnostic shape in those \nleads overlying the right ventricle (V1 and V2). \n“Rabbit Ears”\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 164,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 219,
      "word_count": 34,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "RIGHT BUNDLE BRANCH BLOCK\n\nQRS complex greater than 0.10 sec\n\nQRS axis normal or deviated to the right\n\nBroad S wave in leads I, aVL, V,, and V,\n\nRSR’ pattern in lead V, with R’ taller than R\nqRS pattern in V, and V,\n\nST-T distorted and in opposite direction to term\nportion of QRS (this is not ST elevation or ST\ndepression).\n\nFigure 10.17 @ Right bundle branch block.\n\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 165,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 371,
      "word_count": 70,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Left Bundle Branch Blocks\nWhat QRS morphology is characteristic?\nFor LBBB the wide QRS complex assumes a \ncharacteristic change in shape in those leads \nopposite the left ventricle (right ventricular \nleads - V1 and V2). \nBroad, \ndeep S \nwaves\nNormal\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 166,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 251,
      "word_count": 40,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "EFT BUNDLE BRANCH BLOCK\n\nQRS complex greater than 0.10 sec\nQRS predominantly negative in leads V, and V,\n\nQRS predominantly positive in V, and V, and oft\nnotched\n\nAbsence of small, normal Q waves in I, aVL, V,,\nand V,\n\nWide monophasic R waves in I, aVL, V,, V,, and\nV.\n\n6\n\nFigure 10.16 @ Left bundle branch block.\n\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 167,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 315,
      "word_count": 59,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Summary\nThis Module introduced you to:\n– ST Elevation and Non-ST Elevation MIs \n– Left Ventricular Hypertrophy\n– Bundle Branch Blocks\nDon’t worry too much right now about trying to \nremember all the details. You’ll focus more on \nadvanced ECG interpretation in your clinical \nyears!\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 168,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 283,
      "word_count": 45,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "End of Module VI\nAdvanced 12-Lead \nInterpretation\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 169,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 50,
      "word_count": 7,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "ECG Rhythm Interpretation\nModule VII a\nReading 12-Lead ECG’s\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 170,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 61,
      "word_count": 9,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Course Objective\n• To systematically analyze a 12-lead \nECG.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 171,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 61,
      "word_count": 9,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Learning Modules\n• ECG Basics\n• How to Analyze a Rhythm\n• Normal Sinus Rhythm\n• Heart Arrhythmias\n• Diagnosing a Myocardial Infarction\n• Advanced 12-Lead Interpretation\n• Reading 12-Lead ECG’s\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 172,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 193,
      "word_count": 31,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Reading 12-Lead ECGs\n• The 12-Lead ECG contains information that will assist \nyou in making diagnostic and treatment decisions in your \nclinical practice. In previous modules you learned how to \nread and interpret parts of the ECG. Now, we will bring all \nthat you have learned together so that you can \nsystematically read and interpret a 12-lead ECG.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 173,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 353,
      "word_count": 58,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Reading 12-Lead ECGs\nThe best way to read 12-lead ECGs is to develop a step-\nby-step approach (just as we did for analyzing a rhythm \nstrip). In these modules we present a 6-step approach:\n1. Calculate RATE\n2. Determine RHYTHM\n3. Determine QRS AXIS\n4. Calculate INTERVALS\n5. Assess for HYPERTROPHY\n6. Look for evidence of INFARCTION\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 174,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 333,
      "word_count": 57,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nIf you use the rhythm \nstrip portion of the \n12-lead ECG the total \nlength of it is always \n10 seconds long. So \nyou can count the \nnumber of R waves \nin the rhythm strip \nand multiply by 6 to \ndetermine the beats \nper minute.\nRate?\n12 (R waves) x 6 = 72 bpm\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 175,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 306,
      "word_count": 59,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nTip: the rhythm strip portion of the 12-lead ECG is a good \nplace to look at when trying to determine the rhythm \nbecause the 12 leads only capture a few beats.\nLead II\nRhythm?\nAtrial \nfibrillation\nRhythm strip\n1 of 12 leads\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 176,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 272,
      "word_count": 48,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nAxis refers to the mean QRS axis (or vector) during ventricular \ndepolarization. As you recall when the ventricles depolarize (in a \nnormal heart) the direction of current flows leftward and downward \nbecause most of the ventricular mass is in the left ventricle. We like \nto know the QRS axis because an abnormal axis can suggest \ndisease such as pulmonary hypertension from a pulmonary \nembolism.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 177,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 446,
      "word_count": 70,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nThe QRS axis is determined by overlying a circle, in the frontal \nplane. By convention, the degrees of the circle are as shown.\nThe normal QRS axis lies between -30o and +90o.\n0o\n30o\n-30o\n60o \n-60o\n-90o\n-120o\n90o\n120o\n150o\n180o\n-150o\nA QRS axis that falls between -30o\nand -90o is abnormal and called left \naxis deviation.\nA QRS axis that falls between +90o\nand +150o is abnormal and called \nright axis deviation.\nA QRS axis that falls between +150o\nand -90o is abnormal and called \nsuperior right axis deviation.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 178,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 561,
      "word_count": 99,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nWe can quickly determine whether the QRS axis is \nnormal by looking at leads I and II. \nIf the QRS complex is \noverall positive (R > Q+S)\nin leads I and II, the QRS \naxis is normal.\nQRS negative (R < Q+S)\nIn this ECG what leads \nhave QRS complexes \nthat are negative? \nequivocal?\nQRS equivocal (R = Q+S)\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 179,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 351,
      "word_count": 65,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nHow do we know the axis is normal when the QRS \ncomplexes are positive in leads I and II? \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 180,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 138,
      "word_count": 25,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nThe answer lies in the fact that each frontal lead \ncorresponds to a location on the circle.\n0o\n30o\n-30o\n60o \n-60o\n-90o\n-120o\n90o\n120o\n150o\n180o\n-150o\nI\nII\navF\navL\navR\nLimb leads\nI = +0o\nII = +60o\nIII = +120o\nAugmented leads\navL = -30o\navF = +90o\navR = -150o\nI\nII\nIII\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 181,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 315,
      "word_count": 65,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "0o\n30o\n-30o\n60o \n-60o\n-90o\n-120o\n90o\n120o\n150o\n180o\n-150o\nRate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nSince lead I is orientated at 0o a wave of depolarization directed towards \nit will result in a positive QRS axis. Therefore any mean QRS vector \nbetween -90o and +90o will be positive.\nI\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 182,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 293,
      "word_count": 52,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "0o\n30o\n-30o\n60o \n-60o\n-90o\n-120o\n90o\n120o\n150o\n180o\n-150o\nRate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nSince lead I is orientated at 0o a wave of depolarization directed towards \nit will result in a positive QRS axis. Therefore any mean QRS vector \nbetween -90o and +90o will be positive.\nSimilarly, since lead II is orientated at 60o\na wave of depolarization directed towards \nit will result in a positive QRS axis. \nTherefore any mean QRS vector between \n-30o and +150o will be positive.\nI\nII\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 183,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 497,
      "word_count": 87,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "0o\n30o\n-30o\n60o \n-60o\n-90o\n-120o\n90o\n120o\n150o\n180o\n-150o\nRate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nSince lead I is orientated at 0o a wave of depolarization directed towards \nit will result in a positive QRS axis. Therefore any mean QRS vector \nbetween -90o and +90o will be positive.\nSimilarly, since lead II is orientated at 60o\na wave of depolarization directed towards \nit will result in a positive QRS axis. \nTherefore any mean QRS vector between \n-30o and +150o will be positive.\nTherefore, if the QRS complex is positive \nin both leads I and II the QRS axis must \nbe between -30o and 90o (where leads I \nand II overlap) and, as a result, the axis \nmust be normal.\nI\nII\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 184,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 682,
      "word_count": 124,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "0o\n30o\n-30o\n60o \n-60o\n-90o\n-120o\n90o\n120o\n150o\n180o\n-150o\n0o\n30o\n-30o\n60o \n-60o\n-90o\n-120o\n90o\n120o\n150o\n180o\n-150o\nRate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nNow using what you just learned fill in the following table. For example, if \nthe QRS is positive in lead I and negative in lead II what is the QRS axis? \n(normal, left, right or right superior axis deviation)\nQRS Complexes\nI\nAxis\nI\nII\n+         +\n+          -\nnormal\nleft axis deviation\nII\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 185,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 454,
      "word_count": 84,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "0o\n30o\n-30o\n60o \n-60o\n-90o\n-120o\n90o\n120o\n150o\n180o\n-150o\n0o\n30o\n-30o\n60o \n-60o\n-90o\n-120o\n90o\n120o\n150o\n180o\n-150o\nRate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\n… if the QRS is negative in lead I and positive in lead II what is the QRS \naxis? (normal, left, right or right superior axis deviation)\nQRS Complexes\nI\nAxis\nI\nII\n+         +\n+          -\n-\n+\nnormal\nleft axis deviation\nright axis deviation\nII\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 186,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 406,
      "word_count": 77,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "0o\n30o\n-30o\n60o \n-60o\n-90o\n-120o\n90o\n120o\n150o\n180o\n-150o\nRate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\n… if the QRS is negative in lead I and negative in lead II what is the QRS \naxis? (normal, left, right or right superior axis deviation)\nQRS Complexes\nI\nAxis\nI          II\n+         +\n+          -\n-\n+\n-\n-\nnormal\nleft axis deviation\nright axis deviation\nright superior \naxis deviation\n0o\n30o\n-30o\n60o \n-60o\n-90o\n-120o\n90o\n120o\n150o\n180o\n-150o\nII\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 187,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 450,
      "word_count": 83,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nIs the QRS axis normal in this ECG?\nNo, there is left axis \ndeviation.\nThe QRS is \npositive in I \nand negative \nin II.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 188,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 166,
      "word_count": 30,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nTo summarize:\n– The normal QRS axis falls between -30o and +90o because ventricular \ndepolarization is leftward and downward.\n– Left axis deviation occurs when the axis falls between -30o and -90o.\n– Right axis deviation occurs when the axis falls between +90o and +150o.\n– Right superior axis deviation occurs when the axis falls between between \n+150o and -90o.\nQRS Complexes\nAxis\nI\nII\n+          +\n+          -\n-\n+\n-\n-\nnormal\nleft axis deviation\nright axis deviation\nright superior \naxis deviation\n– A quick way to determine                                         \nthe QRS axis is to look at the \nQRS complexes in leads I \nand II.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 189,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 682,
      "word_count": 111,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SUMMARY Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nTo summarize VII a:\n1. Calculate RATE\n2. Determine RHYTHM\n3. Determine QRS AXIS\n– Normal\n– Left axis deviation\n– Right axis deviation\n– Right superior axis deviation\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 190,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 221,
      "word_count": 36,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SUMMARY Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nIn VII b we will cover the next 3 steps:\n1. Calculate RATE\n2. Determine RHYTHM\n3. Determine QRS AXIS\n4. Calculate INTERVALS\n5. Assess for HYPERTROPHY\n6. Look for evidence of INFARCTION\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 191,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 240,
      "word_count": 40,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\n• Intervals refers to the length of the PR and QT intervals \nand the width of the QRS complexes. You should have \nalready determined the PR and QRS during the “rhythm” \nstep, but if not, do so in this step. \n• In the following few slides we’ll review what is a normal \nand abnormal PR, QRS and QT interval. Also listed are a \nfew common causes of abnormal intervals. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 192,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 415,
      "word_count": 75,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nPR interval\n< 0.12 s\n0.12-0.20 s\n> 0.20 s\nHigh catecholamine \nstates\nWolff-Parkinson-White\nNormal\nAV nodal blocks\nWolff-Parkinson-White\n1st Degree AV Block\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 193,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 203,
      "word_count": 29,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nQRS complex\n< 0.10 s\n0.10-0.12 s\n> 0.12 s\nNormal\nIncomplete bundle \nbranch block\nBundle branch block\nPVC\nVentricular rhythm\nRemember: If you have a BBB determine if it is a right or left \nBBB. If you need a refresher see Module VI.\n3rd degree AV block with \nventricular escape rhythm\nIncomplete bundle branch block\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 194,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 362,
      "word_count": 62,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nQT interval\nThe duration of the QT interval is \nproportionate to the heart rate. The faster \nthe heart beats, the faster the ventricles \nrepolarize so the shorter the QT interval. \nTherefore what is a “normal” QT varies \nwith the heart rate. For each heart rate you \nneed to calculate an adjusted QT interval, \ncalled the “corrected QT” (QTc):\nQTc = QT / square root of RR interval\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 195,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 429,
      "word_count": 73,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nQTc interval\n< 0.44 s\n> 0.44 s\nNormal\nLong QT\nA prolonged QT can be very dangerous. It may predispose an individual to a type of \nventricular tachycardia called Torsades de Pointes. Causes include drugs, electrolyte \nabnormalities, CNS disease, post-MI, and congenital heart disease.\nTorsades de Pointes\nLong QT\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 196,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 359,
      "word_count": 56,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nPR interval?\nQRS width?\nQTc interval?\n0.08 seconds\n0.16 seconds\n0.49 seconds\nQT = 0.40 s\nRR = 0.68 s\nSquare root of \nRR = 0.82\nQTc = 0.40/0.82 \n= 0.49 s\nInterpretation of intervals? Normal PR and QRS, long QT\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 197,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 256,
      "word_count": 47,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nTip: Instead of calculating the QTc, a quick way to estimate if the  \nQT interval long is to use the following rule:\nA QT > half of the RR interval is probably long.\nNormal QT\nLong QT\nQT\nRR\n10 boxes\n23 boxes\n17 boxes\n13 boxes\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 198,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 273,
      "word_count": 53,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nIn this step of the 12-lead ECG analysis, we use the ECG \nto determine if any of the 4 chambers of the heart are \nenlarged or hypertrophied. We want to determine if there \nare any of the following:\n– Right atrial enlargement (RAE)\n– Left atrial enlargement (LAE)\n– Right ventricular hypertrophy (RVH)\n– Left ventricular hypertrophy (LVH)\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 199,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 385,
      "word_count": 64,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\n• In Module VI we introduced the concept of left \nventricular hypertrophy. As you remember the QRS \nvoltage increases with LVH and is characterized by tall \nQRS complexes in certain leads. Similarly for right \nventricular hypertrophy we look at the QRS complexes for \nchanges in voltage patterns.\n• With right and left atrial enlargement we analyze the P \nwave (since the P wave represents atrial depolarization). \nHere we also look for changes in voltage patterns. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 200,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 514,
      "word_count": 81,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nRight atrial enlargement\n– Take a look at this ECG. What do you notice about the P waves?\nThe P waves are tall, especially in leads II, III and avF. \nOuch! They would hurt to sit on!!\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 201,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 231,
      "word_count": 43,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nRight atrial enlargement\n– To diagnose RAE you can use the following criteria:\n• II\nP > 2.5 mm, or\n• V1 or V2\nP > 1.5 mm\nRemember 1 small \nbox in height = 1 mm\nA cause of RAE is RVH from pulmonary hypertension.\n> 2 ½ boxes (in height)\n> 1 ½ boxes (in height)\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 202,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 306,
      "word_count": 64,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nLeft atrial enlargement\n– Take a look at this ECG. What do you notice about the P waves?\nThe P waves in lead II are notched and in lead V1 they \nhave a deep and wide negative component.\nNotched \nNegative deflection\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 203,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 262,
      "word_count": 47,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nLeft atrial enlargement\n– To diagnose LAE you can use the following criteria:\n• II\n> 0.04 s (1 box) between notched peaks, or\n• V1\nNeg. deflection > 1 box wide x 1 box deep\nNormal\nLAE\nA common cause of LAE is LVH from hypertension.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 204,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 279,
      "word_count": 53,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nRight ventricular hypertrophy\n– Take a look at this ECG. What do you notice about the axis and QRS \ncomplexes over the right ventricle (V1, V2)?\nThere is right axis deviation (negative in I, positive in II) and \nthere are tall R waves in V1, V2.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 205,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 293,
      "word_count": 52,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nRight ventricular hypertrophy\n– Compare the R waves in V1, V2 from a normal ECG and one from \na person with RVH.\n– Notice the R wave is normally small in V1, V2 because the right \nventricle does not have a lot of muscle mass.\n– But in the hypertrophied right ventricle the R wave is tall in V1, V2.\nNormal\nRVH\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 206,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 357,
      "word_count": 68,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nRight ventricular hypertrophy\n– To diagnose RVH you can use the following criteria:\n•\nRight axis deviation, and\n• V1\nR wave > 7mm tall\nA common \ncause of RVH \nis left heart \nfailure.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 207,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 230,
      "word_count": 40,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nLeft ventricular hypertrophy\n– Take a look at this ECG. What do you notice about the axis and QRS \ncomplexes over the left ventricle (V5, V6) and right ventricle (V1, V2)?\nThere is left axis deviation (positive in I, negative in II) and there \nare tall R waves in V5, V6 and deep S waves in V1, V2.\nThe deep S waves \nseen in the leads over \nthe right ventricle are \ncreated because the \nheart is depolarizing \nleft, superior and \nposterior (away from \nleads V1, V2). \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 208,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 515,
      "word_count": 92,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nLeft ventricular hypertrophy\n– To diagnose LVH you can use the following criteria*:\n•\nR in V5 (or V6) + S in V1 (or V2) > 35 mm, or\n• avL\nR > 13 mm\nA common cause of LVH \nis hypertension.\n* There are several \nother criteria for the \ndiagnosis of LVH.\nS = 13 mm\nR = 25 mm\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 209,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 318,
      "word_count": 67,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nA 63 yo man has longstanding, uncontrolled hypertension. Is there evidence \nof heart disease from his hypertension? (Hint: There a 3 abnormalities.)\nYes, there is left axis deviation (positive in I, negative in II), left atrial enlargement \n(> 1 x 1 boxes in V1) and LVH (R in V5 = 27 + S in V2 = 10 > 35 mm).\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 210,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 358,
      "word_count": 66,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\n• When analyzing a 12-lead ECG for evidence of an \ninfarction you want to look for the following:\n– Abnormal Q waves\n– ST elevation or depression\n– Peaked, flat or inverted T waves\n• These topics were covered in Modules V and VI where \nyou learned:\n– ST elevation (or depression) of 1 mm in 2 or more \ncontiguous leads is consistent with an AMI\n– There are ST elevation (Q-wave) and non-ST elevation \n(non-Q wave) MIs\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 211,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 465,
      "word_count": 84,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nTip: One way to determine if Q waves (and R waves) are abnormal is by \nlooking at the width and using the following mantra (read red downwards):\nAny   \nAny Q wave in V1\nAny  \nAny Q wave in V2\nAny\nAny Q wave in V3\n20\nA Q wave > 20 msec in V4     (i.e. 0.02 sec or ½ width of a box)\n30\nA Q wave > 30 msec in V5\n30\nA Q wave > 30 msec in V6\n30\nA Q wave > 30 msec in I\n30\nA Q wave > 30 msec in avL\n30\nA Q wave > 30 msec in II\n30\nA Q wave > 30 msec in avF\nR40\nA R wave > 40 msec in V1\nR50\nA R wave > 50 msec in V2\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 212,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 555,
      "word_count": 141,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nThis mantra corresponds to the ECG in the following way:\nAny\nAny\nAny\n20\n30\n30\n30\n30\n30\n30\nR40\nR50\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 213,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 145,
      "word_count": 28,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SUMMARY Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nTo summarize:\n1. Calculate RATE\n2. Determine RHYTHM\n3. Determine QRS AXIS\n– Normal\n– Left axis deviation\n– Right axis deviation\n– Right superior axis deviation\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 214,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 215,
      "word_count": 34,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SUMMARY Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nTo summarize:\n1. Calculate RATE\n2. Determine RHYTHM\n3. Determine QRS AXIS\n4. Calculate INTERVALS\n–\nPR\n–\nQRS\n–\nQT\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 215,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 168,
      "word_count": 28,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SUMMARY Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nTo summarize:\n1. Calculate RATE\n2. Determine RHYTHM\n3. Determine QRS AXIS\n4. Calculate INTERVALS\n5. Assess for HYPERTROPHY\n–\nRight and left atrial enlargement\n–\nRight and left ventricular hypertrophy\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 216,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 255,
      "word_count": 38,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SUMMARY Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nTo summarize:\n1. Calculate RATE\n2. Determine RHYTHM\n3. Determine QRS AXIS\n4. Calculate INTERVALS\n5. Assess for HYPERTROPHY\n6. Look for evidence of INFARCTION\n–\nAbnormal Q waves\n–\nST elevation or depression\n–\nPeaked, flat or inverted T waves\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 217,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 296,
      "word_count": 48,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SUMMARY Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nTo summarize:\n1. Calculate RATE\n2. Determine RHYTHM\n3. Determine QRS AXIS\n4. Calculate INTERVALS\n5. Assess for HYPERTROPHY\n6. Look for evidence of INFARCTION\nNow to finish this module lets analyze a 12-lead ECG!\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 218,
      "type": "list",
      "char_count": 267,
      "word_count": 42,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SUMMARY Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nA 16 yo young man ran into a guardrail while riding a motorcycle. \nIn the ED he is comatose and dyspneic. This is his ECG.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 219,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 178,
      "word_count": 32,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SUMMARY Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nWhat is the rate?\nApprox. 132 bpm  (22 R waves x 6)\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 220,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 107,
      "word_count": 19,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SUMMARY Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nWhat is the rhythm?\nSinus tachycardia\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 221,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 93,
      "word_count": 13,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SUMMARY Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nWhat is the QRS axis? Right axis deviation  (- in I, + in II)\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 222,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 117,
      "word_count": 21,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SUMMARY Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nWhat are the PR, QRS \nand QT intervals?\nPR = 0.12 s, QRS = 0.08 s, QTc = 0.482 s\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 223,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 136,
      "word_count": 27,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SUMMARY Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nIs there evidence of \natrial enlargement?\nNo  (no peaked, notched or negatively  \ndeflected P waves)\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 224,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 156,
      "word_count": 22,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SUMMARY Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nIs there evidence of \nventricular hypertrophy?\nNo  (no tall R waves in V1/V2 or V5/V6)\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 225,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 142,
      "word_count": 22,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SUMMARY Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nInfarct: Are there abnormal \nQ waves?\nYes! In leads V1-V6 and I, avL\nAny\nAny\nAny\n20\n30\n30\n30\n30\n30\n30\nR40\nR50\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 226,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 165,
      "word_count": 32,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SUMMARY Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nInfarct: Is the ST elevation \nor depression?\nYes! Elevation in V2-V6, I and avL. \nDepression in II, III and avF. \n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 227,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 169,
      "word_count": 27,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SUMMARY Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nInfarct: Are there T wave \nchanges?\nNo\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 228,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 94,
      "word_count": 14,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "SUMMARY Rate Rhythm Axis Intervals Hypertrophy Infarct\nECG analysis: Sinus tachycardia at 132 bpm, right axis deviation, \nlong QT, and evidence of ST elevation infarction in the \nanterolateral leads (V1-V6, I, avL)  with reciprocal changes (the \nST depression) in the inferior leads (II, III, avF).\nThis young man suffered an \nacute myocardial infarction after \nblunt trauma. An \nechocardiogram showed \nanteroseptal akinesia in the left \nventricle with severely \ndepressed LV function \n(EF=28%). An angiogram \nshowed total occlusion in the \nproximal LAD with collaterals \nfrom the RCA and LCX.\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 229,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 594,
      "word_count": 87,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
  {
    "text": "End of Module VII \nReading 12-Lead ECGs\n",
    "metadata": {
      "source": "ECG book.pdf",
      "page": 230,
      "chunk_index": 0,
      "total_chunks": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "char_count": 40,
      "word_count": 7,
      "title": "Ecg Book",
      "author": "Unknown Author",
      "year": "2020",
      "edition": ""
    }
  },
